window.LSC_DATA={"timelines":{"ep1":{"id":"ep1","title":"Why does ice cream melt?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":132.758,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.429,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why does ice cream melt?","start":10.93,"end":12.379}]},"look":{"start":13.529,"end":21.643,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Look!","start":13.529,"end":13.921},{"text":"Here is a lovely ice cream cone.","start":14.385,"end":16.377},{"text":"Ooh... it is cold...","start":16.814,"end":18.906},{"text":"and hard.","start":19.462,"end":20.441},{"text":"Burr!","start":20.902,"end":21.593}]},"sunny":{"start":22.543,"end":28.278,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Now... the sun is shining!","start":22.543,"end":24.164},{"text":"It is warm.","start":24.667,"end":26.022},{"text":"Watch... what happens.","start":26.446,"end":28.228}]},"drip":{"start":30.178,"end":35.722,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Drip...","start":30.178,"end":30.57},{"text":"drip...","start":30.777,"end":31.305},{"text":"drip!","start":31.541,"end":31.866},{"text":"The ice cream is turning into liquid.","start":32.073,"end":34.233},{"text":"It is melting!","start":34.688,"end":35.672}]},"why":{"start":36.822,"end":41.984,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Why?","start":36.822,"end":37.414},{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":38.061,"end":39.316},{"text":"Much... much closer.","start":39.722,"end":41.934}]},"bits":{"start":43.284,"end":47.548,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Ice cream is made of tiny, tiny bits...","start":43.284,"end":45.676},{"text":"far too small to see.","start":46.206,"end":47.498}]},"cold":{"start":48.448,"end":57.515,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"When ice cream is cold, the tiny bits hold on to each other... tightly.","start":48.448,"end":52.886},{"text":"So the ice cream stays hard.","start":53.337,"end":54.729},{"text":"We call that...","start":55.188,"end":55.991},{"text":"a solid.","start":56.614,"end":57.465}]},"warm":{"start":58.615,"end":66.566,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"When something warm touches the ice cream...","start":58.615,"end":61.235},{"text":"like the sun, or your warm hand...","start":61.759,"end":64.226},{"text":"it gives the tiny bits heat.","start":64.806,"end":66.516}]},"wiggle":{"start":67.466,"end":74.486,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Heat makes the tiny bits wiggle, and jiggle!","start":67.466,"end":70.244},{"text":"They wiggle so much...","start":70.729,"end":72.17},{"text":"that they let go of each other.","start":72.695,"end":74.436}]},"flow":{"start":75.586,"end":82.741,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Now they can slide, and flow.","start":75.586,"end":77.757},{"text":"The ice cream becomes runny, like a liquid.","start":78.15,"end":81.02},{"text":"That... is melting!","start":81.399,"end":82.691}]},"sayit":{"start":84.041,"end":93.656,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"So, heat makes solid ice cream melt into a liquid.","start":84.041,"end":87.536},{"text":"Say it with me...","start":87.927,"end":88.991},{"text":"Heat makes it melt!","start":89.599,"end":90.533},{"text":"Heat... makes it... melt!","start":91.871,"end":93.606}]},"freeze":{"start":95.556,"end":106.138,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"And guess what?","start":95.556,"end":96.612},{"text":"If we put the melted ice cream back in the freezer...","start":97.246,"end":99.537},{"text":"the cold makes the tiny bits hold hands again, and it turns hard.","start":100.087,"end":104.62},{"text":"It freezes!","start":105.11,"end":106.088}]},"try1":{"start":107.438,"end":111.201,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":107.438,"end":108.507},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for two ice cubes.","start":108.969,"end":111.151}]},"try2":{"start":112.001,"end":119.249,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Put one in the sunshine...","start":112.001,"end":113.334},{"text":"and one in the shade.","start":113.955,"end":115.103},{"text":"Which one melts first?","start":115.534,"end":117.061},{"text":"Watch... and see!","start":117.607,"end":119.199}]},"bye":{"start":121.349,"end":129.358,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":121.349,"end":122.974},{"text":"Remember... heat makes things melt.","start":123.408,"end":125.2},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":125.622,"end":128.214},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":128.678,"end":129.308}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep2":{"id":"ep2","title":"Why does a ball always come down?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":121.761,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":13.06,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why does a ball always come down?","start":10.93,"end":13.01}]},"throw":{"start":14.16,"end":20.167,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Curie throws the ball,","start":14.16,"end":15.252},{"text":"up...","start":15.413,"end":15.855},{"text":"up...","start":16.084,"end":16.526},{"text":"up!","start":16.737,"end":16.969},{"text":"And look.","start":17.19,"end":17.888},{"text":"Down...","start":18.341,"end":18.878},{"text":"it comes.","start":19.115,"end":20.117}]},"again":{"start":21.467,"end":30.712,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try again, higher!","start":21.467,"end":23.08},{"text":"Up...","start":23.583,"end":23.966},{"text":"up...","start":24.188,"end":24.629},{"text":"up!","start":24.87,"end":25.103},{"text":"And...","start":25.332,"end":25.948},{"text":"down.","start":26.177,"end":26.765},{"text":"Every...","start":26.972,"end":27.589},{"text":"single...","start":28.583,"end":29.258},{"text":"time!","start":30.121,"end":30.662}]},"why":{"start":32.012,"end":35.819,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Why doesn't the ball just float away...","start":32.012,"end":34.08},{"text":"into the sky?","start":34.627,"end":35.769}]},"earth":{"start":37.119,"end":44.039,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Here's the secret.","start":37.119,"end":38.057},{"text":"Our Earth...","start":38.51,"end":39.144},{"text":"is enormous!","start":39.705,"end":41.07},{"text":"And it pulls everything towards it.","start":41.56,"end":43.989}]},"gravity":{"start":45.139,"end":49.807,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"This pull has a name...","start":45.139,"end":46.499},{"text":"gravity.","start":47.03,"end":47.576},{"text":"Gravity pulls the ball down.","start":48.062,"end":49.757}]},"feel":{"start":51.107,"end":58.943,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"You can't see gravity.","start":51.107,"end":52.468},{"text":"But you can feel it!","start":52.919,"end":54.069},{"text":"Jump up...","start":54.572,"end":55.314},{"text":"and gravity brings you back down.","start":55.937,"end":58.088},{"text":"Thump!","start":58.551,"end":58.893}]},"pulls":{"start":60.243,"end":68.392,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Gravity pulls the ball.","start":60.243,"end":61.699},{"text":"Gravity pulls a leaf.","start":62.078,"end":63.526},{"text":"Gravity pulls the rain.","start":63.946,"end":65.331},{"text":"Gravity pulls you...","start":65.75,"end":66.872},{"text":"and me!","start":67.409,"end":68.342}]},"feet":{"start":69.492,"end":75.167,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Gravity even keeps your feet on the ground, so you don't float away.","start":69.492,"end":73.433},{"text":"Thank you, gravity!","start":73.876,"end":75.117}]},"together":{"start":76.267,"end":83.657,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Gravity pulls everything, big or small.","start":76.267,"end":79.019},{"text":"A big ball and a small ball drop together...","start":79.412,"end":82.103},{"text":"and land together!","start":82.627,"end":83.607}]},"sayit":{"start":85.157,"end":92.476,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":85.157,"end":86.221},{"text":"Gravity pulls things down!","start":86.761,"end":88.352},{"text":"Gravity... pulls things... down!","start":89.734,"end":92.426}]},"try1":{"start":94.376,"end":99.837,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":94.376,"end":95.445},{"text":"Hold a ball in one hand, and a toy in the other...","start":95.931,"end":98.273},{"text":"at the same height.","start":98.822,"end":99.787}]},"try2":{"start":100.737,"end":106.369,"hold":1.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Let go at the same time.","start":100.737,"end":102.6},{"text":"Which one lands first?","start":103.022,"end":104.544},{"text":"Watch closely!","start":105.177,"end":106.319}]},"bye":{"start":108.669,"end":118.361,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":108.669,"end":110.294},{"text":"Remember... gravity pulls everything down to the Earth.","start":110.786,"end":114.277},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":114.67,"end":117.262},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":117.681,"end":118.311}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep3":{"id":"ep3","title":"Where does rain come from?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":141.332,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.256,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Where does rain come from?","start":10.93,"end":12.206}]},"rain":{"start":13.356,"end":18.735,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Pitter patter, pitter patter.","start":13.356,"end":14.748},{"text":"It's raining!","start":15.172,"end":15.963},{"text":"But where was all this water... before?","start":16.442,"end":18.685}]},"sea":{"start":20.035,"end":26.509,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's find out.","start":20.035,"end":20.977},{"text":"Here is a big, blue sea.","start":21.436,"end":23.458},{"text":"And here...","start":23.882,"end":24.473},{"text":"is the warm sun.","start":25.108,"end":26.459}]},"warm":{"start":27.609,"end":34.369,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"The sun warms the water.","start":27.609,"end":29.027},{"text":"And when water gets warm...","start":29.423,"end":30.654},{"text":"tiny bits of it float up, into the air.","start":31.277,"end":34.319}]},"vapour":{"start":35.469,"end":43.613,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"They are so tiny, you can't see them.","start":35.469,"end":38.111},{"text":"This is called...","start":38.564,"end":39.555},{"text":"water vapour.","start":40.116,"end":40.979},{"text":"Up...","start":41.469,"end":41.852},{"text":"up...","start":42.052,"end":42.493},{"text":"up they go!","start":42.696,"end":43.563}]},"cold":{"start":44.713,"end":52.823,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"High up in the sky, it is very cold.","start":44.713,"end":47.354},{"text":"Burr!","start":47.738,"end":48.428},{"text":"The tiny bits of water get cold...","start":48.914,"end":51.006},{"text":"and huddle together.","start":51.579,"end":52.773}]},"cloud":{"start":53.923,"end":59.986,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"They make tiny droplets.","start":53.923,"end":55.395},{"text":"Millions of tiny droplets together make...","start":55.847,"end":58.538},{"text":"a cloud!","start":59.173,"end":59.936}]},"join":{"start":61.286,"end":68.097,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"The droplets bump into each other, and join up.","start":61.286,"end":64.106},{"text":"They get bigger...","start":64.485,"end":65.227},{"text":"and bigger...","start":65.807,"end":66.617},{"text":"and heavier.","start":67.196,"end":68.047}]},"fall":{"start":69.197,"end":74.249,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"When the drops get too heavy to float...","start":69.197,"end":71.189},{"text":"down they fall.","start":71.8,"end":72.891},{"text":"That's rain!","start":73.307,"end":74.199}]},"river":{"start":75.549,"end":82.239,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The rain fills the rivers, and the rivers run back to the sea.","start":75.549,"end":79.605},{"text":"Then the sun warms the water... again!","start":79.998,"end":82.189}]},"cycle":{"start":83.539,"end":92.916,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Up as vapour... cloud... rain...","start":83.539,"end":86.418},{"text":"back to the sea.","start":87.051,"end":87.947},{"text":"Round and round it goes.","start":88.534,"end":90.006},{"text":"We call this...","start":90.406,"end":91.299},{"text":"the water cycle!","start":91.882,"end":92.866}]},"sayit":{"start":94.416,"end":103.921,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":94.416,"end":95.48},{"text":"The sun lifts the water up, and the rain brings it down!","start":96.092,"end":99.533},{"text":"Sun lifts it up...","start":100.869,"end":101.756},{"text":"rain brings it down!","start":102.48,"end":103.871}]},"try1":{"start":105.821,"end":111.487,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":105.821,"end":106.89},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a glass of very cold water, with ice.","start":107.352,"end":111.437}]},"try2":{"start":112.387,"end":125.234,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Leave it on the table, and","start":112.387,"end":114.296},{"text":"wait...","start":114.467,"end":114.831},{"text":"Look!","start":115.773,"end":116.165},{"text":"Little drops appear on the outside of the glass.","start":116.584,"end":119.089},{"text":"That's water vapour from the air, turning back into water.","start":119.497,"end":123.447},{"text":"Just like a tiny cloud!","start":123.848,"end":125.184}]},"bye":{"start":127.134,"end":137.932,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":127.134,"end":128.759},{"text":"Remember... the sun lifts water up to make clouds, and clouds give us rain.","start":129.193,"end":133.774},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":134.196,"end":136.787},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":137.252,"end":137.882}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep4":{"id":"ep4","title":"Why do I have a shadow?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":132.628,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.363,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why do I have a shadow?","start":10.93,"end":12.313}]},"walk":{"start":13.463,"end":20.834,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"It's a sunny day.","start":13.463,"end":14.496},{"text":"Curie is walking...","start":14.92,"end":16.081},{"text":"and look!","start":16.686,"end":17.401},{"text":"Something dark is following Curie, on the ground.","start":17.842,"end":20.784}]},"wave":{"start":22.134,"end":30.106,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"It's a shadow!","start":22.134,"end":22.955},{"text":"When Curie waves...","start":23.457,"end":24.83},{"text":"the shadow waves.","start":25.415,"end":26.551},{"text":"When Curie jumps...","start":27.055,"end":28.262},{"text":"the shadow jumps!","start":28.867,"end":30.056}]},"torch":{"start":31.406,"end":36.285,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Where does a shadow come from?","start":31.406,"end":33.095},{"text":"Let's find out...","start":33.662,"end":34.653},{"text":"with a torch.","start":35.276,"end":36.235}]},"light":{"start":37.585,"end":43.262,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Light travels in straight lines, like tiny, speedy arrows.","start":37.585,"end":42.268},{"text":"Zoom!","start":42.721,"end":43.212}]},"wall":{"start":44.562,"end":47.775,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"When the light hits the wall...","start":44.562,"end":45.787},{"text":"the wall is bright.","start":46.318,"end":47.725}]},"block":{"start":48.875,"end":56.147,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Now Curie stands in front of the torch.","start":48.875,"end":51.021},{"text":"The light hits Curie...","start":51.473,"end":52.464},{"text":"and stops.","start":53.099,"end":54.015},{"text":"It can't go through Curie!","start":54.509,"end":56.097}]},"shadow":{"start":57.247,"end":63.437,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Behind Curie, no light can reach the wall.","start":57.247,"end":60.403},{"text":"That dark spot...","start":60.782,"end":61.665},{"text":"is Curie's shadow.","start":62.245,"end":63.387}]},"define":{"start":64.737,"end":67.598,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"A shadow is the place where the light can't go.","start":64.737,"end":67.548}]},"lowhigh":{"start":69.098,"end":78.015,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Watch this!","start":69.098,"end":70.04},{"text":"When the sun is low...","start":70.47,"end":71.312},{"text":"the shadow is very, very long.","start":71.836,"end":74.228},{"text":"When the sun is high...","start":74.784,"end":75.725},{"text":"the shadow is short!","start":76.331,"end":77.965}]},"noon":{"start":79.315,"end":85.459,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"That's why your shadow is long in the morning and evening...","start":79.315,"end":81.901},{"text":"and short at noon, when the sun is high.","start":82.441,"end":85.409}]},"sayit":{"start":86.759,"end":96.41,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":86.759,"end":87.823},{"text":"A shadow is where the light can't go!","start":88.435,"end":91.119},{"text":"A shadow...","start":92.455,"end":93.297},{"text":"is where the light...","start":94.021,"end":94.881},{"text":"can't go!","start":95.624,"end":96.36}]},"try1":{"start":98.31,"end":106.052,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":98.31,"end":99.379},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a torch.","start":99.841,"end":101.428},{"text":"Make the room dark,","start":101.871,"end":103.977},{"text":"and shine it on the wall.","start":104.148,"end":106.002}]},"try2":{"start":106.952,"end":117.188,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Put your hand in the light.","start":106.952,"end":108.427},{"text":"Can you make a dog?","start":108.877,"end":110.074},{"text":"A bird?","start":110.635,"end":111.325},{"text":"Move your hand closer to the torch.","start":111.872,"end":113.658},{"text":"Does the shadow get bigger...","start":114.066,"end":115.407},{"text":"or smaller?","start":115.961,"end":117.138}]},"bye":{"start":119.288,"end":129.228,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":119.288,"end":120.913},{"text":"Remember... your body blocks the light, and that makes a shadow.","start":121.347,"end":125.069},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":125.491,"end":128.083},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":128.547,"end":129.178}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep5":{"id":"ep5","title":"Why do boats float?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":131.352,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.292,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why do boats float?","start":10.93,"end":12.242}]},"stone":{"start":13.392,"end":20.887,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Here's a big pond.","start":13.392,"end":14.384},{"text":"Splash!","start":14.808,"end":15.7},{"text":"Curie drops in a stone.","start":16.179,"end":17.57},{"text":"Down...","start":17.973,"end":18.51},{"text":"it goes.","start":18.73,"end":19.422},{"text":"It sinks.","start":19.659,"end":20.837}]},"boat":{"start":22.187,"end":27.104,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Now Curie puts in a little toy boat.","start":22.187,"end":24.195},{"text":"It stays on top.","start":24.654,"end":25.645},{"text":"It floats!","start":26.069,"end":27.054}]},"why":{"start":28.404,"end":31.915,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Why does the stone sink...","start":28.404,"end":30.021},{"text":"but the boat floats?","start":30.568,"end":31.865}]},"push":{"start":33.215,"end":41.278,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Here's the secret...","start":33.215,"end":34.057},{"text":"water pushes up!","start":34.682,"end":35.544},{"text":"Try pushing a ball under the water in the bath.","start":35.989,"end":38.587},{"text":"Can you feel the water pushing it back up?","start":39.034,"end":41.228}]},"displace":{"start":42.578,"end":48.675,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"When something sits in water, it pushes some water out of the way.","start":42.578,"end":45.693},{"text":"And the water...","start":46.078,"end":47.123},{"text":"pushes back up!","start":47.735,"end":48.625}]},"stone2":{"start":49.975,"end":59.24,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The stone is small, and heavy.","start":49.975,"end":51.897},{"text":"It pushes only a little water away...","start":52.349,"end":54.421},{"text":"so the water's push is too small.","start":55.055,"end":56.897},{"text":"Down...","start":57.391,"end":57.927},{"text":"it sinks.","start":58.149,"end":59.19}]},"boat2":{"start":60.54,"end":70.463,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The boat is wide, and hollow, full of air.","start":60.54,"end":63.085},{"text":"It pushes a lot of water away!","start":63.464,"end":65.231},{"text":"So the water pushes up hard, and holds the boat up.","start":65.691,"end":69.009},{"text":"It floats!","start":69.428,"end":70.413}]},"clay1":{"start":71.763,"end":77.834,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's test it.","start":71.763,"end":72.707},{"text":"Curie squashes some clay into a ball.","start":73.15,"end":75.412},{"text":"Plop!","start":75.792,"end":76.134},{"text":"It sinks.","start":76.606,"end":77.784}]},"clay2":{"start":79.134,"end":86.486,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Now Curie makes the same clay into a wide boat shape...","start":79.134,"end":82.156},{"text":"It floats!","start":82.999,"end":83.983},{"text":"Same clay...","start":84.399,"end":85.091},{"text":"different shape!","start":85.666,"end":86.436}]},"sayit":{"start":87.986,"end":94.903,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":87.986,"end":89.05},{"text":"Water pushes up!","start":89.59,"end":90.673},{"text":"Water... pushes... up!","start":92.055,"end":94.853}]},"try1":{"start":96.803,"end":107.765,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":96.803,"end":97.872},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a bowl of water.","start":98.358,"end":100.458},{"text":"Collect a spoon, a coin, a cork, a plastic lid...","start":100.856,"end":105.769},{"text":"and a leaf.","start":106.386,"end":107.715}]},"try2":{"start":108.665,"end":115.135,"hold":1.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Guess first...","start":108.665,"end":109.557},{"text":"will it sink, or float?","start":110.138,"end":111.722},{"text":"Then drop it in.","start":112.207,"end":113.786},{"text":"Were you right?","start":114.237,"end":115.085}]},"bye":{"start":117.435,"end":127.952,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":117.435,"end":119.06},{"text":"Remember... water pushes up, and that is why boats float.","start":119.552,"end":123.869},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":124.262,"end":126.853},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":127.272,"end":127.902}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep6":{"id":"ep6","title":"What makes things happen?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":158.759,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.381,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"What makes things happen?","start":10.93,"end":12.331}]},"seed":{"start":13.481,"end":20.478,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Curie has a tiny seed...","start":13.481,"end":14.892},{"text":"and Curie wants a flower!","start":15.478,"end":16.701},{"text":"But a seed on its own...","start":17.18,"end":18.881},{"text":"just sits there.","start":19.437,"end":20.428}]},"pieces":{"start":21.778,"end":29.301,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"A flower needs lots of pieces.","start":21.778,"end":23.949},{"text":"Soil!","start":24.408,"end":25.137},{"text":"A seed!","start":25.601,"end":26.698},{"text":"Water!","start":27.202,"end":27.89},{"text":"And sunshine!","start":28.37,"end":29.251}]},"pie":{"start":30.401,"end":37.046,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Each piece is a slice of Curie's cause pie.","start":30.401,"end":33.393},{"text":"When the pie is full...","start":33.79,"end":35.381},{"text":"something will happen!","start":36.004,"end":36.996}]},"wait":{"start":38.346,"end":46.273,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Now... we wait.","start":38.346,"end":40.138},{"text":"Causes take time.","start":40.591,"end":42.378},{"text":"One day...","start":42.784,"end":43.408},{"text":"two days...","start":44.026,"end":44.717},{"text":"three days...","start":45.244,"end":46.223}]},"grow":{"start":47.373,"end":54.739,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Pop!","start":47.373,"end":47.714},{"text":"A little sprout!","start":48.135,"end":48.977},{"text":"And...","start":49.462,"end":50.078},{"text":"a flower!","start":50.651,"end":51.193},{"text":"All the pieces together...","start":51.673,"end":53.062},{"text":"made it happen!","start":53.698,"end":54.689}]},"test":{"start":56.239,"end":63.281,"hold":0.7,"chunks":[{"text":"Which pieces really matter?","start":56.239,"end":57.674},{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":58.319,"end":59.761},{"text":"Take one piece away...","start":60.264,"end":61.306},{"text":"and see what happens.","start":61.928,"end":63.231}]},"nowater":{"start":64.481,"end":72.641,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"No water?","start":64.481,"end":65.239},{"text":"Nothing grows.","start":66.18,"end":67.216},{"text":"No flower!","start":67.637,"end":68.428},{"text":"Water makes a difference...","start":68.888,"end":70.466},{"text":"so water is a cause.","start":71.003,"end":72.591}]},"nosun":{"start":73.941,"end":80.942,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"No sunshine?","start":73.941,"end":75.083},{"text":"Just a tiny, white sprout.","start":76.115,"end":77.857},{"text":"No flower!","start":78.236,"end":79.028},{"text":"Sunshine is a cause too.","start":79.5,"end":80.892}]},"teddy":{"start":82.242,"end":87.352,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Hmm.","start":82.242,"end":82.584},{"text":"Teddy sat next to the pot all week.","start":82.977,"end":85.18},{"text":"Did teddy make the flower grow?","start":85.56,"end":87.302}]},"teddytest":{"start":88.452,"end":96.174,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's test!","start":88.452,"end":89.344},{"text":"Take teddy away...","start":89.772,"end":90.764},{"text":"the flower still grows!","start":91.371,"end":92.963},{"text":"Teddy is not a cause.","start":93.401,"end":94.682},{"text":"It was just there!","start":95.105,"end":96.124}]},"rule":{"start":97.574,"end":104.485,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"So... to make something happen, you need all the pieces.","start":97.574,"end":101.166},{"text":"To stop it...","start":101.609,"end":102.401},{"text":"just take one piece away!","start":102.95,"end":104.435}]},"hands":{"start":105.885,"end":116.223,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Doctors use this trick!","start":105.885,"end":107.296},{"text":"Germs on your hands and fingers in your mouth can make a tummy ache.","start":107.758,"end":111.333},{"text":"Wash the germs away...","start":111.776,"end":112.917},{"text":"and that tummy ache can't happen!","start":113.54,"end":116.173}]},"sayit":{"start":117.523,"end":124.81,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":117.523,"end":118.587},{"text":"Lots of pieces make it happen!","start":119.207,"end":120.799},{"text":"Lots of pieces...","start":122.129,"end":123.32},{"text":"make it happen!","start":123.94,"end":124.76}]},"try1":{"start":126.71,"end":134.646,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":126.71,"end":127.779},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for two cups, cotton wool...","start":128.214,"end":130.548},{"text":"and two bean seeds.","start":131.13,"end":132.676},{"text":"Put a seed in each cup.","start":133.141,"end":134.596}]},"try2":{"start":135.546,"end":144.032,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Water one cup every day.","start":135.546,"end":137.261},{"text":"Keep the other one dry.","start":137.664,"end":139.019},{"text":"Wait a few days.","start":139.442,"end":140.501},{"text":"Which one grows?","start":140.895,"end":142.25},{"text":"Which piece was missing?","start":142.798,"end":143.982}]},"bye":{"start":146.132,"end":155.359,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":146.132,"end":147.757},{"text":"Remember... lots of pieces together make things happen.","start":148.231,"end":151.172},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":151.593,"end":154.184},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":154.679,"end":155.309}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep7":{"id":"ep7","title":"Why do magnets stick?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":149.725,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.399,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why do magnets stick?","start":10.93,"end":12.349}]},"fridge":{"start":13.499,"end":20.466,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Look!","start":13.499,"end":13.891},{"text":"Curie made a drawing.","start":14.355,"end":15.338},{"text":"She puts it on the fridge...","start":15.775,"end":16.867},{"text":"with a magnet.","start":17.423,"end":18.114},{"text":"Click!","start":18.575,"end":19},{"text":"It sticks!","start":19.496,"end":20.416}]},"door":{"start":21.566,"end":28.135,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Now Curie tries the magnet on the wooden door...","start":21.566,"end":23.958},{"text":"It does not stick!","start":24.592,"end":25.575},{"text":"It falls off,","start":26.039,"end":27.318},{"text":"plop!","start":27.493,"end":28.085}]},"why":{"start":29.435,"end":34.833,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Why does a magnet stick to some things...","start":29.435,"end":31.659},{"text":"but not to others?","start":32.206,"end":33.501},{"text":"Let's find out!","start":33.995,"end":34.783}]},"clip":{"start":36.133,"end":41.808,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":36.133,"end":37.575},{"text":"Here is a paper clip...","start":38.071,"end":39.112},{"text":"Zip!","start":39.673,"end":40.044},{"text":"The magnet pulls it.","start":40.534,"end":41.758}]},"nothing":{"start":42.808,"end":49.601,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"A pencil... nothing.","start":42.808,"end":44.695},{"text":"A leaf... nothing.","start":45.079,"end":46.942},{"text":"A plastic block... nothing.","start":47.385,"end":49.551}]},"iron":{"start":50.701,"end":56.219,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"The paper clip has...","start":50.701,"end":52.043},{"text":"iron in it!","start":52.667,"end":53.3},{"text":"Magnets pull things with iron in them.","start":53.803,"end":56.169}]},"foil":{"start":57.619,"end":66.114,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"Kitchen foil is shiny metal too...","start":57.619,"end":59.561},{"text":"but the magnet does not pull it!","start":60.084,"end":62.214},{"text":"Magnets pull iron...","start":62.675,"end":64.29},{"text":"not every metal.","start":64.87,"end":66.064}]},"gap":{"start":67.514,"end":74.613,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"A magnet can pull a paper clip...","start":67.514,"end":69.543},{"text":"without even touching it!","start":70.155,"end":71.429},{"text":"The pull is invisible.","start":71.845,"end":73.132},{"text":"We cannot see it!","start":73.563,"end":74.563}]},"ends":{"start":75.913,"end":80.772,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Every magnet has two ends.","start":75.913,"end":77.99},{"text":"A red end...","start":78.383,"end":79.018},{"text":"and a blue end.","start":79.543,"end":80.722}]},"pull":{"start":81.772,"end":87.91,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Watch!","start":81.772,"end":82.414},{"text":"A red end and a blue end come close...","start":82.842,"end":85.334},{"text":"Click!","start":85.941,"end":86.366},{"text":"They pull together.","start":86.804,"end":87.86}]},"push":{"start":88.91,"end":96.074,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"Now turn one around.","start":88.91,"end":90.3},{"text":"Red and red...","start":90.743,"end":91.725},{"text":"they push apart!","start":92.274,"end":93.258},{"text":"The same ends push each other away.","start":93.747,"end":96.024}]},"home":{"start":97.474,"end":107.349,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Now we know why!","start":97.474,"end":98.466},{"text":"The fridge door has iron in it...","start":98.927,"end":100.572},{"text":"so the magnet sticks.","start":101.184,"end":102.956},{"text":"Wood has no iron in it...","start":103.45,"end":105.06},{"text":"so the magnet falls off.","start":105.583,"end":107.299}]},"sayit":{"start":108.649,"end":115.318,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":108.649,"end":109.713},{"text":"Magnets pull iron!","start":110.333,"end":111.511},{"text":"Magnets... pull... iron!","start":112.842,"end":115.268}]},"try1":{"start":117.218,"end":126.523,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":117.218,"end":118.287},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a fridge magnet.","start":118.722,"end":120.537},{"text":"Find a paper clip, a pencil, a leaf...","start":120.959,"end":124.618},{"text":"and some foil.","start":125.204,"end":126.473}]},"try2":{"start":127.423,"end":134.848,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Guess first...","start":127.423,"end":128.315},{"text":"will it stick?","start":128.871,"end":129.754},{"text":"Then test it.","start":130.217,"end":131.008},{"text":"Keep magnets out of your mouth!","start":131.402,"end":132.644},{"text":"Which things does the magnet pull?","start":133.064,"end":134.798}]},"bye":{"start":136.948,"end":146.325,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":136.948,"end":138.573},{"text":"Remember... magnets pull things with iron in them.","start":139.047,"end":142.138},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":142.559,"end":145.15},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":145.645,"end":146.275}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep8":{"id":"ep8","title":"How do we hear sounds?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":141.261,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.824,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"How do we hear sounds?","start":10.93,"end":12.774}]},"twang":{"start":13.924,"end":20.287,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Curie has a box guitar.","start":13.924,"end":15.51},{"text":"Listen...","start":15.934,"end":16.513},{"text":"Twang!","start":17.117,"end":17.871},{"text":"Where does that sound come from?","start":18.812,"end":20.237}]},"closer":{"start":21.587,"end":25.123,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":21.587,"end":22.842},{"text":"Much... much closer!","start":23.301,"end":25.073}]},"string":{"start":26.023,"end":31.596,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Look at the string!","start":26.023,"end":27.039},{"text":"It is wiggling...","start":27.473,"end":28.456},{"text":"back and forth...","start":29.079,"end":30.209},{"text":"super fast!","start":30.755,"end":31.546}]},"wiggle":{"start":33.096,"end":38.495,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"When something wiggles like that...","start":33.096,"end":34.642},{"text":"it makes a sound.","start":35.267,"end":36.259},{"text":"Sounds are made by wiggles!","start":36.704,"end":38.445}]},"air":{"start":39.795,"end":50.511,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The wiggle pushes the air...","start":39.795,"end":41.502},{"text":"and the air passes it along.","start":42.032,"end":43.927},{"text":"Push...","start":44.413,"end":44.951},{"text":"push...","start":45.524,"end":46.263},{"text":"push...","start":46.869,"end":47.607},{"text":"all the way...","start":48.293,"end":48.985},{"text":"to your ear!","start":49.569,"end":50.461}]},"ear":{"start":51.811,"end":57.536,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Inside your ear is a tiny drum.","start":51.811,"end":53.926},{"text":"The wiggle makes it wiggle too...","start":54.377,"end":55.714},{"text":"and you hear the sound!","start":56.348,"end":57.486}]},"feel":{"start":58.836,"end":66.576,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"You can feel a wiggle!","start":58.836,"end":59.911},{"text":"Gently put your fingers on your throat...","start":60.326,"end":62.117},{"text":"and hum.","start":62.697,"end":63.492},{"text":"Hmmm.","start":63.951,"end":64.511},{"text":"Can you feel it buzz?","start":64.993,"end":66.526}]},"test":{"start":68.076,"end":71.722,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":68.076,"end":69.518},{"text":"Let's put some rice on a drum.","start":70.003,"end":71.672}]},"soft":{"start":72.622,"end":79.348,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"A tiny tap...","start":72.622,"end":73.969},{"text":"makes tiny wiggles.","start":74.512,"end":75.848},{"text":"The rice hops a little...","start":76.264,"end":77.397},{"text":"and the sound is quiet.","start":77.973,"end":79.298}]},"loud":{"start":80.448,"end":86.876,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"A big bang...","start":80.448,"end":81.429},{"text":"makes big wiggles!","start":81.968,"end":83.392},{"text":"The rice jumps way up...","start":83.874,"end":85.094},{"text":"and the sound is loud!","start":85.646,"end":86.826}]},"highlow":{"start":88.176,"end":99.81,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Now let's change how fast it wiggles.","start":88.176,"end":90.368},{"text":"A short string wiggles faster...","start":90.811,"end":92.786},{"text":"and makes a high sound.","start":93.335,"end":95.419},{"text":"A long string wiggles slower...","start":95.909,"end":97.496},{"text":"and makes a low sound.","start":98.13,"end":99.76}]},"sayit":{"start":101.31,"end":107.797,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":101.31,"end":102.374},{"text":"Sounds are wiggles!","start":102.955,"end":104.17},{"text":"Sounds... are... wiggles!","start":105.555,"end":107.747}]},"try1":{"start":109.697,"end":115.418,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":109.697,"end":110.766},{"text":"Ask a grown-up to stretch a rubber band...","start":111.258,"end":113.277},{"text":"over an open box.","start":113.82,"end":115.368}]},"try2":{"start":116.318,"end":127.095,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Pluck it gently...","start":116.318,"end":117.304},{"text":"away from your face.","start":117.852,"end":119.241},{"text":"Can you see it wiggle?","start":119.703,"end":120.844},{"text":"Now stretch it tighter, with your grown-up.","start":121.45,"end":124.003},{"text":"Is the sound higher...","start":124.385,"end":125.602},{"text":"or lower?","start":126.166,"end":127.045}]},"bye":{"start":129.195,"end":137.861,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":129.195,"end":130.82},{"text":"Remember... sounds are made by wiggles.","start":131.261,"end":133.735},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":134.158,"end":136.749},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":137.181,"end":137.811}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep9":{"id":"ep9","title":"Why is it dark at night?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":142.077,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.261,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why is it dark at night?","start":10.93,"end":12.211}]},"evening":{"start":13.361,"end":20.125,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"It's evening.","start":13.361,"end":14.418},{"text":"The sun goes down...","start":14.842,"end":16.016},{"text":"and down...","start":16.62,"end":17.421},{"text":"and it gets dark.","start":17.976,"end":19.181},{"text":"Good night!","start":19.642,"end":20.075}]},"morning":{"start":21.525,"end":25.471,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"In the morning...","start":21.525,"end":22.246},{"text":"the sun comes up again.","start":22.88,"end":24.272},{"text":"Good morning!","start":24.736,"end":25.421}]},"why":{"start":26.771,"end":31.845,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Does the sun go to sleep at night?","start":26.771,"end":28.544},{"text":"Let's fly up into space...","start":29.111,"end":30.502},{"text":"and find out!","start":31.125,"end":31.795}]},"earth":{"start":32.945,"end":40.556,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Here is our Earth, a giant ball.","start":32.945,"end":35.506},{"text":"And here is the sun.","start":35.959,"end":37.27},{"text":"The sun doesn't go to sleep.","start":37.676,"end":39.017},{"text":"It keeps on shining!","start":39.464,"end":40.506}]},"half":{"start":41.856,"end":50.127,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"The sun can only shine on one side of the Earth.","start":41.856,"end":44.548},{"text":"That side has day.","start":44.932,"end":46.351},{"text":"The other side is dark...","start":46.795,"end":48.387},{"text":"it has night.","start":48.959,"end":50.077}]},"spin":{"start":51.227,"end":56.055,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"And our Earth is always spinning...","start":51.227,"end":53.59},{"text":"slowly, round and round.","start":54.213,"end":56.005}]},"house":{"start":57.055,"end":62.518,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Look... here's Curie's house!","start":57.055,"end":58.797},{"text":"Right now it faces the sun.","start":59.212,"end":61.061},{"text":"It's daytime!","start":61.481,"end":62.468}]},"turn":{"start":63.618,"end":73.482,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"The Earth turns...","start":63.618,"end":65.151},{"text":"and turns.","start":65.762,"end":67.255},{"text":"Now Curie's house faces away from the sun.","start":67.671,"end":70.122},{"text":"It's night!","start":70.553,"end":71.258},{"text":"The sun is still shining.","start":71.741,"end":73.432}]},"oneday":{"start":74.882,"end":81.004,"hold":0.7,"chunks":[{"text":"One whole spin takes one day...","start":74.882,"end":77.063},{"text":"and one night.","start":77.606,"end":78.409},{"text":"Then it starts all over again!","start":78.825,"end":80.954}]},"other":{"start":82.204,"end":86.867,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"So when it's day for you...","start":82.204,"end":83.796},{"text":"it's night for children on the other side of the world!","start":84.336,"end":86.817}]},"sky":{"start":88.367,"end":97.945,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"In the garden, the sun seems to come up...","start":88.367,"end":90.536},{"text":"and go down.","start":91.149,"end":91.939},{"text":"But the sun isn't really going up and down.","start":92.375,"end":94.397},{"text":"We are turning!","start":94.844,"end":95.986},{"text":"The Earth spins.","start":96.489,"end":97.895}]},"sayit":{"start":99.445,"end":105.96,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":99.445,"end":100.509},{"text":"The Earth spins!","start":101.09,"end":102.477},{"text":"The Earth... spins!","start":103.862,"end":105.91}]},"try1":{"start":107.86,"end":114.856,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":107.86,"end":108.929},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a torch and a ball.","start":109.421,"end":111.952},{"text":"Put a sticker on the ball...","start":112.345,"end":113.486},{"text":"that's your house.","start":114.015,"end":114.806}]},"try2":{"start":115.756,"end":126.243,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Make the room dark.","start":115.756,"end":117.046},{"text":"Shine the torch on the ball...","start":117.443,"end":118.884},{"text":"never in eyes!","start":119.467,"end":120.459},{"text":"Turn the ball slowly.","start":120.923,"end":122.246},{"text":"When is it day at your house?","start":122.628,"end":124.516},{"text":"When is it night?","start":125.099,"end":126.193}]},"bye":{"start":128.343,"end":138.677,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":128.343,"end":129.968},{"text":"Remember... the Earth spins, and that makes day and night.","start":130.409,"end":134.55},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":134.973,"end":137.565},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":137.996,"end":138.627}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep10":{"id":"ep10","title":"Where does the sugar go?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":140.38,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.407,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Where does the sugar go?","start":10.93,"end":12.357}]},"stir":{"start":13.507,"end":19.192,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Curie is making lemonade!","start":13.507,"end":15.094},{"text":"In goes a spoon of sugar.","start":15.559,"end":17.012},{"text":"Stir... stir... stir!","start":17.449,"end":19.142}]},"gone":{"start":20.192,"end":23.847,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Look!","start":20.192,"end":20.584},{"text":"The sugar is gone!","start":21.087,"end":22.266},{"text":"Where did it go?","start":22.731,"end":23.797}]},"taste":{"start":25.347,"end":31.074,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Curie tastes a drop...","start":25.347,"end":26.824},{"text":"Yum!","start":27.372,"end":27.714},{"text":"It's sweet!","start":28.208,"end":28.999},{"text":"So the sugar must still be there.","start":29.432,"end":31.024}]},"closer":{"start":32.374,"end":38.248,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"But why can't we see it?","start":32.374,"end":33.677},{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":34.325,"end":35.579},{"text":"Much... much closer.","start":35.985,"end":38.198}]},"bits":{"start":39.548,"end":44.701,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"A grain of sugar is made of tiny, tiny bits.","start":39.548,"end":42.705},{"text":"Just like ice cream, remember?","start":43.089,"end":44.651}]},"spread":{"start":45.701,"end":52.185,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"The water bits pull the sugar bits apart.","start":45.701,"end":48.256},{"text":"Then the sugar bits spread out...","start":48.707,"end":50.299},{"text":"between the water bits.","start":50.933,"end":52.135}]},"dissolve":{"start":53.285,"end":58.507,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Now they are too tiny to see.","start":53.285,"end":54.927},{"text":"We say...","start":55.306,"end":56.372},{"text":"the sugar dissolves!","start":56.952,"end":58.457}]},"notmelt":{"start":60.207,"end":68.238,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Dissolving is not melting!","start":60.207,"end":61.484},{"text":"Melting needs heat, like the sun.","start":61.969,"end":64.792},{"text":"Dissolving is tiny bits spreading out, in water.","start":65.172,"end":68.188}]},"test":{"start":69.738,"end":75.517,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":69.738,"end":71.18},{"text":"Does everything dissolve?","start":71.61,"end":73.733},{"text":"Let's try sand.","start":74.276,"end":75.467}]},"sand":{"start":76.517,"end":84.352,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Stir... stir...","start":76.517,"end":79.105},{"text":"The sand sinks to the bottom, and stays there.","start":79.645,"end":82.228},{"text":"Sand doesn't dissolve!","start":82.71,"end":84.302}]},"salt":{"start":85.852,"end":93.804,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Salt dissolves too.","start":85.852,"end":87.044},{"text":"And if the salty water dries up in the sun...","start":87.487,"end":90.309},{"text":"the salt comes back!","start":90.859,"end":91.85},{"text":"It was there all along.","start":92.34,"end":93.754}]},"sayit":{"start":95.304,"end":102.79,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"So where did the sugar go?","start":95.304,"end":96.744},{"text":"Say it with me...","start":97.345,"end":98.409},{"text":"It's still there!","start":99.021,"end":99.863},{"text":"It's still... there!","start":101.257,"end":102.74}]},"try1":{"start":104.69,"end":111.558,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":104.69,"end":105.759},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for two glasses of water, a spoon of sugar...","start":106.251,"end":109.285},{"text":"and a spoon of sand.","start":109.828,"end":111.508}]},"try2":{"start":112.458,"end":118.695,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Stir the sugar into one glass...","start":112.458,"end":114.3},{"text":"and the sand into the other.","start":114.848,"end":116.59},{"text":"Which one disappears?","start":117.052,"end":118.645}]},"try3":{"start":119.995,"end":125.403,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Taste only the sugar water, and only if your grown-up says yes.","start":119.995,"end":123.808},{"text":"Is the sugar still there?","start":124.211,"end":125.353}]},"bye":{"start":127.503,"end":136.98,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":127.503,"end":129.128},{"text":"Remember... when sugar dissolves, it's still there.","start":129.602,"end":132.793},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":133.214,"end":135.805},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":136.3,"end":136.93}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep11":{"id":"ep11","title":"Why do rolling things stop?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":136.722,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.84,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why do rolling things stop?","start":10.93,"end":12.79}]},"roll":{"start":13.94,"end":23.474,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Curie rolls her toy car across the floor.","start":13.94,"end":16.326},{"text":"Wheee!","start":16.75,"end":17.192},{"text":"It rolls...","start":17.671,"end":18.795},{"text":"and rolls...","start":19.351,"end":20.329},{"text":"and slows down...","start":20.91,"end":21.882},{"text":"and stops.","start":22.508,"end":23.424}]},"why":{"start":24.774,"end":29.33,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Why did it stop?","start":24.774,"end":26.078},{"text":"Nobody grabbed it!","start":26.734,"end":27.56},{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":28.025,"end":29.28}]},"rub":{"start":30.63,"end":38.191,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Here's the secret...","start":30.63,"end":31.472},{"text":"The wheels rub on the floor, as they roll.","start":32.019,"end":34.525},{"text":"The rubbing pulls back on the car...","start":35.019,"end":36.604},{"text":"and slows it down.","start":37.15,"end":38.141}]},"name":{"start":39.191,"end":42.443,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists call this...","start":39.191,"end":40.634},{"text":"rubbing friction.","start":41.259,"end":42.393}]},"test":{"start":43.743,"end":50.082,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":43.743,"end":45.185},{"text":"Same ramp...","start":45.606,"end":46.588},{"text":"same car...","start":47.201,"end":48.092},{"text":"but different floors.","start":48.665,"end":50.032}]},"carpet":{"start":51.082,"end":56.073,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"On bumpy carpet...","start":51.082,"end":52.064},{"text":"there is lots of rubbing.","start":52.687,"end":54.229},{"text":"The car stops quickly!","start":54.732,"end":56.023}]},"floor":{"start":57.173,"end":62.404,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"On a smooth wooden floor...","start":57.173,"end":58.556},{"text":"there is less rubbing.","start":59.079,"end":60.471},{"text":"The car rolls further.","start":60.931,"end":62.354}]},"ice":{"start":63.504,"end":70.973,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"On slippery ice...","start":63.504,"end":64.677},{"text":"there is hardly any rubbing!","start":65.288,"end":66.723},{"text":"The car rolls a long, long way...","start":67.139,"end":69.278},{"text":"before it stops.","start":69.873,"end":70.923}]},"grip":{"start":72.473,"end":78.462,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"And friction is useful, too!","start":72.473,"end":74.191},{"text":"It helps your shoes grip the ground...","start":74.622,"end":76.792},{"text":"so you don't slip.","start":77.317,"end":78.412}]},"slip":{"start":79.362,"end":85.592,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"But on slippery ice...","start":79.362,"end":80.865},{"text":"Whoops!","start":81.405,"end":81.836},{"text":"There is hardly any rubbing, so Curie slips and slides!","start":82.318,"end":85.542}]},"brakes":{"start":86.692,"end":91.941,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Bike brakes use rubbing, too.","start":86.692,"end":88.423},{"text":"They rub the wheel...","start":88.866,"end":89.858},{"text":"to slow the bike down.","start":90.407,"end":91.891}]},"sayit":{"start":93.041,"end":101.139,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":93.041,"end":94.105},{"text":"Rubbing slows it down!","start":94.686,"end":96.478},{"text":"Rubbing... slows it... down!","start":97.863,"end":101.089}]},"try1":{"start":103.039,"end":109.939,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":103.039,"end":104.108},{"text":"Ask a grown-up to help you make a ramp, with a big book.","start":104.6,"end":108.158},{"text":"Roll a toy car down it.","start":108.551,"end":109.889}]},"try2":{"start":110.839,"end":122.223,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Where does it stop on the floor?","start":110.839,"end":112.474},{"text":"Put a block there!","start":113.041,"end":113.933},{"text":"Now lay a towel at the bottom...","start":114.395,"end":115.882},{"text":"and roll the car again, from the same spot.","start":116.468,"end":119.413},{"text":"Where does it stop... now?","start":119.832,"end":122.173}]},"bye":{"start":124.323,"end":133.322,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":124.323,"end":125.948},{"text":"Remember... rubbing slows things down.","start":126.389,"end":129.196},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":129.619,"end":132.21},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":132.642,"end":133.272}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep17":{"id":"ep17","title":"Where do rainbows come from?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":132.982,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.416,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Where do rainbows come from?","start":10.93,"end":12.366}]},"s1":{"start":13.516,"end":19.792,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The rain has stopped.","start":13.516,"end":14.926},{"text":"The sun is peeking out.","start":15.35,"end":16.942},{"text":"And look...","start":17.379,"end":18.285},{"text":"a rainbow!","start":18.841,"end":19.742}]},"look":{"start":21.092,"end":22.397,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":21.092,"end":22.347}]},"e1":{"start":23.697,"end":30.369,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Sunlight looks white...","start":23.697,"end":25.139},{"text":"but it has a secret.","start":25.686,"end":26.825},{"text":"It is all the colours...","start":27.319,"end":28.897},{"text":"mixed together!","start":29.442,"end":30.319}]},"e2":{"start":31.369,"end":42.06,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"A prism is a glass triangle.","start":31.369,"end":33.452},{"text":"When light goes through it...","start":33.905,"end":35.225},{"text":"the colours spread out!","start":35.785,"end":36.927},{"text":"Red, orange, yellow, green...","start":37.417,"end":39.458},{"text":"blue, and violet!","start":39.985,"end":42.01}]},"e3":{"start":43.16,"end":46.904,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Raindrops can do it too!","start":43.16,"end":44.652},{"text":"Every drop is like a tiny prism.","start":45.073,"end":46.854}]},"e4":{"start":47.804,"end":55.849,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Light goes into the drop...","start":47.804,"end":49.71},{"text":"and bends.","start":50.333,"end":51.487},{"text":"It bounces off the back...","start":51.99,"end":53.404},{"text":"and bends again, on the way out!","start":54.027,"end":55.799}]},"e5":{"start":56.949,"end":65.014,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists call this bending... refraction.","start":56.949,"end":60.307},{"text":"Each colour bends a tiny bit differently...","start":60.685,"end":63.393},{"text":"so they fan out!","start":63.973,"end":64.964}]},"e6":{"start":66.114,"end":70.636,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Millions of raindrops send their colours to your eyes...","start":66.114,"end":69.006},{"text":"and you see a rainbow!","start":69.617,"end":70.586}]},"test":{"start":72.136,"end":76.866,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":72.136,"end":73.578},{"text":"Is the sun in front of me...","start":74.008,"end":75.35},{"text":"or behind me?","start":75.874,"end":76.816}]},"t1":{"start":77.766,"end":84.668,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Facing the sun...","start":77.766,"end":78.682},{"text":"no rainbow.","start":79.222,"end":80.148},{"text":"Sun behind me, rain in front...","start":80.631,"end":83.375},{"text":"there it is!","start":83.927,"end":84.618}]},"fact":{"start":86.168,"end":92.657,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Wow fact!","start":86.168,"end":88.032},{"text":"A rainbow is really a whole circle.","start":88.518,"end":90.485},{"text":"The ground hides the bottom part.","start":90.883,"end":92.607}]},"sayit":{"start":94.157,"end":101.649,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":94.157,"end":95.221},{"text":"Raindrops split the sunlight!","start":95.802,"end":97.622},{"text":"Raindrops... split the sunlight!","start":99.007,"end":101.599}]},"try1":{"start":103.549,"end":110.514,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":103.549,"end":104.618},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a spray bottle of water...","start":105.11,"end":107.094},{"text":"on a sunny morning, or late afternoon.","start":107.637,"end":110.464}]},"try2":{"start":111.414,"end":117.969,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Stand with the sun behind you...","start":111.414,"end":113.006},{"text":"and spray a fine mist in front of you.","start":113.554,"end":115.871},{"text":"Can you make your own rainbow?","start":116.334,"end":117.919}]},"bye":{"start":120.069,"end":129.582,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":120.069,"end":121.694},{"text":"Remember... raindrops split the sunlight into colours!","start":122.135,"end":125.416},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":125.879,"end":128.47},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":128.902,"end":129.532}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep18":{"id":"ep18","title":"Why does the Moon change shape?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":153.398,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":13.084,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why does the Moon change shape?","start":10.93,"end":13.034}]},"s1":{"start":14.184,"end":17.131,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"I look at the Moon from my window...","start":14.184,"end":15.704},{"text":"every night.","start":16.29,"end":17.081}]},"s2":{"start":17.931,"end":26.29,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Some nights it is a thin curve.","start":17.931,"end":20.023},{"text":"Some nights it is half.","start":20.482,"end":22.074},{"text":"And some nights...","start":22.498,"end":23.616},{"text":"it is a big, round circle!","start":24.251,"end":26.24}]},"look":{"start":27.59,"end":31.325,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Is the Moon really changing?","start":27.59,"end":29.454},{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":30.02,"end":31.275}]},"e1":{"start":32.625,"end":39.734,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"The Moon does not make its own light.","start":32.625,"end":34.797},{"text":"Sunlight shines on it...","start":35.25,"end":36.54},{"text":"and bounces off, into our eyes.","start":37.1,"end":39.684}]},"e2":{"start":40.734,"end":47.994,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"The sun always lights up half of the Moon.","start":40.734,"end":43.279},{"text":"That half is the Moon's day.","start":43.663,"end":45.098},{"text":"The other half is dark...","start":45.541,"end":46.525},{"text":"the Moon's night.","start":47.098,"end":47.944}]},"e3":{"start":49.094,"end":53.862,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"The Moon travels around the Earth.","start":49.094,"end":50.902},{"text":"Its path is called...","start":51.354,"end":52.424},{"text":"an orbit.","start":53.058,"end":53.812}]},"e4":{"start":54.862,"end":59.898,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"As the Moon goes around...","start":54.862,"end":56.284},{"text":"we see different amounts of its bright half.","start":56.807,"end":59.848}]},"e5":{"start":60.798,"end":66.465,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"When the dark half faces us...","start":60.798,"end":62.644},{"text":"we cannot see the Moon.","start":63.255,"end":64.668},{"text":"That is a new Moon.","start":65.084,"end":66.415}]},"e6":{"start":67.465,"end":74.829,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Then we see a thin crescent...","start":67.465,"end":69.028},{"text":"then half...","start":69.571,"end":70.334},{"text":"then a full Moon, when all of its bright half faces us!","start":70.858,"end":74.779}]},"e7":{"start":75.929,"end":82.944,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Then it shrinks back again.","start":75.929,"end":77.296},{"text":"These shapes are the Moon's phases...","start":77.687,"end":79.771},{"text":"and they repeat about every twenty-nine and a half days!","start":80.379,"end":82.894}]},"test":{"start":84.444,"end":92.988,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it, with a model!","start":84.444,"end":86.941},{"text":"A lamp is the sun...","start":87.427,"end":88.791},{"text":"a ball is the Moon...","start":89.34,"end":90.736},{"text":"and my head is the Earth.","start":91.353,"end":92.938}]},"t1":{"start":93.888,"end":101.959,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"I hold up the ball...","start":93.888,"end":94.872},{"text":"and turn around slowly.","start":95.453,"end":96.977},{"text":"The bright part grows...","start":97.462,"end":98.848},{"text":"and shrinks.","start":99.471,"end":100.453},{"text":"Just like the Moon!","start":100.868,"end":101.909}]},"fact":{"start":103.459,"end":111.292,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Wow fact!","start":103.459,"end":105.323},{"text":"You can often see the Moon in the daytime, too.","start":105.815,"end":108.284},{"text":"The sun lights it up...","start":108.677,"end":110.08},{"text":"even then!","start":110.609,"end":111.242}]},"sayit":{"start":112.792,"end":122.135,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":112.792,"end":113.856},{"text":"The Moon stays round...","start":114.404,"end":115.642},{"text":"our view changes!","start":116.225,"end":117.544},{"text":"The Moon stays round...","start":118.909,"end":120.147},{"text":"our view changes!","start":120.765,"end":122.085}]},"try1":{"start":124.035,"end":129.284,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":124.035,"end":125.104},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a lamp and a ball...","start":125.545,"end":127.633},{"text":"in a dark room.","start":128.217,"end":129.234}]},"try2":{"start":130.184,"end":137.829,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Hold the ball up high, a little above your head...","start":130.184,"end":132.871},{"text":"and turn around slowly.","start":133.469,"end":134.993},{"text":"Can you see the Moon's phases on your ball?","start":135.453,"end":137.779}]},"bye":{"start":139.929,"end":149.998,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":139.929,"end":141.554},{"text":"Remember... the Moon stays round, our view changes!","start":141.977,"end":145.805},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":146.289,"end":148.881},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":149.318,"end":149.948}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep19":{"id":"ep19","title":"How do we know what made something happen?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":173.132,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":13.556,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"How do we know what made something happen?","start":10.93,"end":13.506}]},"s1":{"start":14.656,"end":18.941,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Today is race day!","start":14.656,"end":16.027},{"text":"I am wearing my new spotty socks.","start":16.492,"end":18.891}]},"s2":{"start":19.841,"end":29.507,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Ready...","start":19.841,"end":20.302},{"text":"Steady...","start":21.136,"end":21.728},{"text":"Go!","start":22.513,"end":22.955},{"text":"I win!","start":25.058,"end":26.145},{"text":"My lucky socks...","start":26.624,"end":27.816},{"text":"made me win!","start":28.419,"end":29.457}]},"look":{"start":30.807,"end":33.567,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Did they really?","start":30.807,"end":31.695},{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":32.262,"end":33.517}]},"e1":{"start":34.867,"end":40.496,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"To know if the socks helped...","start":34.867,"end":36.359},{"text":"we need to know...","start":36.984,"end":37.963},{"text":"what would have happened without them.","start":38.524,"end":40.446}]},"e2":{"start":41.596,"end":50.4,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Imagine two of me...","start":41.596,"end":43.039},{"text":"in the same race, at the same time.","start":43.57,"end":46.212},{"text":"One wears spotty socks.","start":46.698,"end":48.388},{"text":"One wears plain socks.","start":48.803,"end":50.35}]},"e3":{"start":51.4,"end":59.925,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"If we both finish together...","start":51.4,"end":52.942},{"text":"the socks made no difference.","start":53.565,"end":55.357},{"text":"If spotty-socks me finishes first...","start":55.86,"end":58.274},{"text":"the socks helped!","start":58.897,"end":59.875}]},"e4":{"start":61.025,"end":67.515,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists call this...","start":61.025,"end":62.468},{"text":"the counterfactual.","start":62.991,"end":64.224},{"text":"What would have happened, if things had been different.","start":64.684,"end":67.465}]},"e5":{"start":68.615,"end":77.42,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Comparing two of me, the same in every way except the socks, is the perfect test.","start":68.615,"end":73.561},{"text":"Scientists call it...","start":74.004,"end":74.995},{"text":"the counterfactual ideal.","start":75.52,"end":77.37}]},"e6":{"start":78.52,"end":84.047,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"But there is only one me!","start":78.52,"end":80.051},{"text":"I cannot run the same race twice, at the same time.","start":80.482,"end":83.997}]},"e7":{"start":85.147,"end":91.67,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"So scientists find the next best thing...","start":85.147,"end":87.639},{"text":"another group of runners, just like me, in plain socks.","start":88.179,"end":91.62}]},"e8":{"start":92.67,"end":98.203,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"The two groups should be alike in every way that matters...","start":92.67,"end":95.695},{"text":"except the socks.","start":96.307,"end":98.153}]},"test":{"start":99.703,"end":111.71,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":99.703,"end":101.145},{"text":"Put all the runners' names in a hat...","start":101.606,"end":104.477},{"text":"and pick them at random, without looking.","start":105.089,"end":107.729},{"text":"Half get spotty socks...","start":108.223,"end":109.795},{"text":"half get plain socks.","start":110.318,"end":111.66}]},"t1":{"start":112.71,"end":121.387,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"With lots of runners, picking at random makes the two groups alike.","start":112.71,"end":116.856},{"text":"Then they race...","start":117.305,"end":118.459},{"text":"and both groups are just as fast!","start":119.002,"end":121.337}]},"t2":{"start":122.687,"end":126.336,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"So the spotty socks...","start":122.687,"end":124.309},{"text":"did not seem to make a difference.","start":124.857,"end":126.286}]},"real":{"start":127.636,"end":132.027,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Doctors test new medicines this way...","start":127.636,"end":130.044},{"text":"to see if they really help.","start":130.6,"end":131.977}]},"sayit":{"start":133.527,"end":141.217,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":133.527,"end":134.591},{"text":"Ask what would happen without it!","start":135.188,"end":137.398},{"text":"Ask what would happen...","start":138.759,"end":139.744},{"text":"without it!","start":140.475,"end":141.167}]},"try1":{"start":143.117,"end":147.537,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":143.117,"end":144.186},{"text":"Ask a grown-up to help you fold a paper plane.","start":144.609,"end":147.487}]},"try2":{"start":148.437,"end":158.365,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Throw it five times.","start":148.437,"end":149.629},{"text":"Then put a sticker on it...","start":150.056,"end":151.42},{"text":"and throw it five times again, the same way, from the same spot.","start":152.046,"end":156.077},{"text":"Does the sticker make a difference?","start":156.573,"end":158.315}]},"bye":{"start":160.465,"end":169.732,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":160.465,"end":162.09},{"text":"Remember... ask what would happen without it!","start":162.508,"end":165.539},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":165.98,"end":168.571},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":169.052,"end":169.682}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep20":{"id":"ep20","title":"How did a doctor test what was making people sick?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":190.394,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":13.883,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"How did a doctor test what was making people sick?","start":10.93,"end":13.833}]},"tb":{"start":14.983,"end":21.905,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"Biscuit and I hop into my Time Bubble.","start":14.983,"end":17.388},{"text":"Set the dial to...","start":17.812,"end":19.004},{"text":"eighteen fifty-four!","start":19.608,"end":20.823},{"text":"Whoosh!","start":21.264,"end":21.855}]},"s1":{"start":23.305,"end":30.879,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"We land in London, long, long ago.","start":23.305,"end":25.897},{"text":"Lots of people there were very ill.","start":26.356,"end":28.295},{"text":"The illness was called... cholera.","start":28.719,"end":30.829}]},"s2":{"start":31.779,"end":36.292,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"It upsets your tummy...","start":31.779,"end":33.1},{"text":"and makes your body lose lots of water.","start":33.648,"end":36.242}]},"s3":{"start":37.192,"end":43.01,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Most doctors thought it spread through bad, smelly air.","start":37.192,"end":40.246},{"text":"They called it... miasma.","start":40.699,"end":42.96}]},"sniff":{"start":43.91,"end":48.514,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Biscuit sniffs lots of smells.","start":43.91,"end":46.147},{"text":"But are smells really making people ill?","start":46.532,"end":48.464}]},"look":{"start":49.514,"end":55.503,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"One doctor...","start":49.514,"end":50.456},{"text":"John Snow...","start":51.079,"end":52.081},{"text":"thought it was the water!","start":52.715,"end":53.705},{"text":"Let's look closer.","start":54.199,"end":55.453}]},"e1":{"start":56.803,"end":63.594,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Water came to people's homes through pipes, under the streets.","start":56.803,"end":60.48},{"text":"So many pipes...","start":60.859,"end":62.025},{"text":"what a tangle!","start":62.606,"end":63.544}]},"test":{"start":65.294,"end":71.698,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"How could he test his idea?","start":65.294,"end":66.71},{"text":"In parts of London...","start":67.342,"end":68.532},{"text":"two water companies sent water down the same streets.","start":69.057,"end":71.648}]},"t1":{"start":72.698,"end":81.058,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"One took its water from lower down the river, where the city's dirty water flowed in.","start":72.698,"end":77.288},{"text":"The other took cleaner water...","start":77.682,"end":79.273},{"text":"from higher up the river.","start":79.797,"end":81.008}]},"drops":{"start":82.058,"end":89.87,"hold":0.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's pretend two water drops race through the pipes!","start":82.058,"end":84.85},{"text":"Muddy Drop, from the dirty water...","start":85.278,"end":86.933},{"text":"and Splashy Drop, from the cleaner water!","start":87.541,"end":89.82}]},"t2":{"start":90.57,"end":101.131,"hold":0.7,"chunks":[{"text":"Pop!","start":90.57,"end":90.911},{"text":"They land in next-door houses.","start":91.397,"end":93.188},{"text":"Next door...","start":93.586,"end":94.314},{"text":"but different water!","start":94.932,"end":96.04},{"text":"Rich or poor...","start":96.543,"end":97.609},{"text":"the people were alike, except for their water.","start":98.243,"end":101.081}]},"which":{"start":102.331,"end":108.257,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"John Snow had to find out which company brought water to each home.","start":102.331,"end":106.203},{"text":"But lots of people didn't even know!","start":106.624,"end":108.207}]},"count":{"start":109.357,"end":116.981,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Then he counted...","start":109.357,"end":110.296},{"text":"and counted...","start":110.916,"end":111.872},{"text":"and counted.","start":112.415,"end":113.344},{"text":"Biscuit tries to catch the counting marks!","start":113.763,"end":116.092},{"text":"Phew!","start":116.539,"end":116.931}]},"t3":{"start":117.981,"end":123.959,"hold":0.9,"chunks":[{"text":"Across all the homes...","start":117.981,"end":119.248},{"text":"cholera struck about six times as often, where the water was dirty!","start":119.796,"end":123.909}]},"t4":{"start":125.359,"end":133.676,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists call this...","start":125.359,"end":126.802},{"text":"a natural experiment.","start":127.358,"end":128.765},{"text":"Two groups, alike in every way that matters...","start":129.228,"end":132.114},{"text":"except one.","start":132.658,"end":133.626}]},"real":{"start":134.976,"end":139.189,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"John Snow called it an experiment...","start":134.976,"end":137.262},{"text":"on the grandest scale!","start":137.86,"end":139.139}]},"back":{"start":140.289,"end":147.289,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Back into the Time Bubble!","start":140.289,"end":141.698},{"text":"Next time, Biscuit and I go back to find another clue...","start":142.121,"end":145.776},{"text":"at a water pump!","start":146.387,"end":147.239}]},"sayit":{"start":148.789,"end":157.505,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":148.789,"end":149.853},{"text":"Alike in every way, except one!","start":150.443,"end":153.213},{"text":"Alike in every way...","start":154.609,"end":155.885},{"text":"except one!","start":156.618,"end":157.455}]},"try1":{"start":159.405,"end":167.861,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":159.405,"end":160.474},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for two cups of water that look the same.","start":160.893,"end":163.981},{"text":"The grown-up secretly stirs a pinch of sugar into one.","start":164.384,"end":167.811}]},"try2":{"start":168.761,"end":175.559,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Take a sip from each.","start":168.761,"end":169.902},{"text":"They are alike in every way, except one.","start":170.298,"end":173.639},{"text":"Which cup has the sugar?","start":174.058,"end":175.509}]},"bye":{"start":177.659,"end":186.994,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":177.659,"end":179.284},{"text":"Remember... alike in every way, except one!","start":179.745,"end":182.737},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":183.238,"end":185.83},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":186.314,"end":186.944}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep21":{"id":"ep21","title":"Why can't we take big bottles or sharp things on a plane?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":170.883,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":15.104,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why can't we take big bottles, or sharp things, on a plane?","start":10.93,"end":15.054}]},"s1":{"start":16.204,"end":24.606,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"We're flying today!","start":16.204,"end":17.246},{"text":"I packed my bag...","start":17.71,"end":18.809},{"text":"my teddy...","start":19.414,"end":20.205},{"text":"a big bottle of water...","start":20.761,"end":22.255},{"text":"and my pointy scissors.","start":22.837,"end":24.556}]},"s2":{"start":25.706,"end":34.652,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"At the airport, the security officer smiles.","start":25.706,"end":28.488},{"text":"Sorry, Curie!","start":28.947,"end":29.938},{"text":"The big bottle and the pointy scissors...","start":30.403,"end":32.194},{"text":"can't come in your cabin bag.","start":32.829,"end":34.602}]},"look":{"start":35.752,"end":38.655,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Why not?","start":35.752,"end":36.504},{"text":"Let's look inside the scanner.","start":37.071,"end":38.605}]},"e1":{"start":39.955,"end":47.497,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Every bag goes through an X-ray scanner.","start":39.955,"end":42.613},{"text":"X-rays are a kind of invisible light...","start":43.066,"end":45.333},{"text":"that can pass through things.","start":45.894,"end":47.447}]},"test":{"start":48.597,"end":56.345,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":48.597,"end":50.039},{"text":"Let's send a teddy...","start":50.46,"end":51.551},{"text":"a pair of scissors...","start":52.164,"end":53.579},{"text":"and a bottle of water, through.","start":54.152,"end":56.295}]},"t1":{"start":57.345,"end":66.587,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The teddy looks pale...","start":57.345,"end":58.587},{"text":"X-rays pass right through it.","start":59.21,"end":60.985},{"text":"The scissor blades look dark...","start":61.488,"end":62.923},{"text":"metal blocks most of the X-rays.","start":63.546,"end":66.537}]},"e4":{"start":67.887,"end":77.428,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Sharp things, like scissors and knives, are easy to spot.","start":67.887,"end":72.278},{"text":"In the cabin, people sit close together...","start":72.657,"end":74.798},{"text":"and a sharp thing could hurt someone.","start":75.379,"end":77.378}]},"e5":{"start":78.428,"end":82.576,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"So sharp things travel in the big suitcase...","start":78.428,"end":80.87},{"text":"under the plane.","start":81.481,"end":82.526}]},"l1":{"start":83.876,"end":90.489,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Liquids are trickier.","start":83.876,"end":84.98},{"text":"On the X-ray picture...","start":85.373,"end":86.652},{"text":"water, juice and shampoo all look alike.","start":87.176,"end":90.439}]},"l2":{"start":91.489,"end":98.49,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"And a few liquids can be harmful...","start":91.489,"end":93.055},{"text":"or catch fire easily.","start":93.595,"end":95.189},{"text":"The scanner can't always tell which is which.","start":95.671,"end":98.44}]},"l3":{"start":99.59,"end":110.011,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"So in the cabin, bottles must be small...","start":99.59,"end":101.774},{"text":"one hundred millilitres or less, less than half a cup.","start":102.387,"end":106.196},{"text":"They all go in one small, clear bag.","start":106.632,"end":109.961}]},"l4":{"start":111.011,"end":117.027,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Small bottles are easier to keep safe...","start":111.011,"end":113.203},{"text":"and the clear bag lets the officers check every bottle.","start":113.784,"end":116.977}]},"real":{"start":118.327,"end":129.632,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Wow fact!","start":118.327,"end":120.191},{"text":"Some airports now have new scanners that take three-D pictures.","start":120.683,"end":124.325},{"text":"They can check liquids better...","start":124.718,"end":126.368},{"text":"so at some of them, bigger bottles are allowed.","start":126.897,"end":129.582}]},"sayit":{"start":131.132,"end":139.368,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":131.132,"end":132.196},{"text":"Safe bags make safe flights!","start":132.744,"end":134.685},{"text":"Safe bags...","start":136.048,"end":137.189},{"text":"make safe flights!","start":137.876,"end":139.318}]},"try1":{"start":141.268,"end":146.437,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":141.268,"end":142.337},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a torch...","start":142.778,"end":144.366},{"text":"and make the room dark.","start":144.95,"end":146.387}]},"try2":{"start":147.337,"end":156.151,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Shine it at a wall...","start":147.337,"end":148.329},{"text":"through a sheet of paper...","start":148.926,"end":150.075},{"text":"a plastic bottle of water...","start":150.655,"end":152.17},{"text":"and a spoon.","start":152.794,"end":153.79},{"text":"Which ones let the light through?","start":154.221,"end":156.101}]},"bye":{"start":158.251,"end":167.483,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":158.251,"end":159.876},{"text":"Remember... safe bags make safe flights!","start":160.299,"end":163.29},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":163.774,"end":166.366},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":166.803,"end":167.433}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep22":{"id":"ep22","title":"Why do we burp?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":158.063,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.395,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why do we burp?","start":10.93,"end":12.345}]},"s1":{"start":13.495,"end":20.154,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Picnic time!","start":13.495,"end":14.487},{"text":"I'm sipping fizzy lemonade...","start":14.951,"end":16.386},{"text":"through a straw.","start":16.99,"end":17.872},{"text":"Sip, sip, sip... gulp!","start":18.313,"end":20.104}]},"s2":{"start":20.954,"end":25.865,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Oops!","start":22.254,"end":22.788},{"text":"Excuse me!","start":23.291,"end":24.064},{"text":"That was a burp-a-saurus!","start":24.528,"end":25.815}]},"guess":{"start":26.865,"end":31.634,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Where did that come from?","start":26.865,"end":27.983},{"text":"Is there a tiny frog...","start":28.549,"end":30.006},{"text":"in my tummy?","start":30.629,"end":31.584}]},"look":{"start":32.734,"end":35.552,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"No frog!","start":32.734,"end":33.626},{"text":"Let's look inside a tummy.","start":34.122,"end":35.502}]},"e1":{"start":36.852,"end":44.669,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"When we eat and drink...","start":36.852,"end":38.194},{"text":"we swallow little bubbles of air.","start":38.725,"end":40.516},{"text":"Gulping too fast...","start":41.002,"end":42.594},{"text":"swallows extra air.","start":43.166,"end":44.619}]},"e2":{"start":45.569,"end":49.642,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"And fizzy drinks...","start":45.569,"end":47.027},{"text":"are full of bubbles of gas.","start":47.65,"end":49.592}]},"e3":{"start":50.642,"end":55.693,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"The bubbles float up, to the top of the stomach...","start":50.642,"end":52.929},{"text":"the bag where our food goes.","start":53.452,"end":55.643}]},"e4":{"start":56.593,"end":67.386,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Then the extra gas is pushed up, out of the stomach.","start":56.593,"end":60.301},{"text":"It rushes up the food tube, the esophagus...","start":60.743,"end":63.625},{"text":"and out of the mouth!","start":64.15,"end":65.177},{"text":"That's a burp!","start":66.649,"end":67.336}]},"e5":{"start":69.086,"end":74.445,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"But why the funny noise?","start":69.086,"end":70.67},{"text":"At the top of the food tube...","start":71.232,"end":72.7},{"text":"there's a tight ring.","start":73.224,"end":74.395}]},"e6":{"start":75.245,"end":81.407,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"The gas squeezes through...","start":75.245,"end":76.683},{"text":"and the top of the tube wobbles...","start":77.223,"end":79.158},{"text":"like the neck of a balloon!","start":79.765,"end":81.357}]},"test":{"start":83.307,"end":90.876,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":83.307,"end":84.749},{"text":"One bottle of plain water...","start":85.234,"end":86.64},{"text":"one bottle of fizzy water...","start":87.189,"end":88.675},{"text":"and a balloon on each.","start":89.293,"end":90.826}]},"t1":{"start":91.776,"end":100.322,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Swirl, swirl!","start":91.776,"end":92.868},{"text":"The plain water balloon stays flat.","start":93.329,"end":95.405},{"text":"The fizzy one...","start":95.848,"end":96.529},{"text":"puffs up a little!","start":97.152,"end":98.036},{"text":"The fizz really is a gas.","start":98.451,"end":100.272}]},"r1":{"start":101.622,"end":108.484,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Babies burp too!","start":101.622,"end":102.704},{"text":"Grown-ups pat a baby's back, to help the burp come out...","start":103.197,"end":106.097},{"text":"so the baby's tummy feels better.","start":106.64,"end":108.434}]},"r2":{"start":109.484,"end":118.635,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Burps are normal.","start":109.484,"end":110.582},{"text":"Everybody burps!","start":110.979,"end":111.97},{"text":"Slow sips mean fewer burps.","start":112.432,"end":114.474},{"text":"And when you burp...","start":114.898,"end":115.516},{"text":"cover your mouth, and say, excuse me!","start":116.043,"end":118.585}]},"sayit":{"start":119.935,"end":127.386,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":119.935,"end":120.999},{"text":"Air goes in, burps come out!","start":121.555,"end":123.547},{"text":"Air goes in...","start":124.91,"end":125.91},{"text":"burps come out!","start":126.548,"end":127.336}]},"try1":{"start":129.286,"end":133.681,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":129.286,"end":130.355},{"text":"Ask a grown-up...","start":130.829,"end":131.655},{"text":"to blow up a balloon.","start":132.236,"end":133.631}]},"try2":{"start":134.581,"end":143.08,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Stretch its neck...","start":134.581,"end":135.422},{"text":"and let the air squeak out, slowly.","start":135.955,"end":138.358},{"text":"Can you hear the wobble...","start":139.842,"end":141.439},{"text":"just like a burp?","start":141.989,"end":143.03}]},"bye":{"start":145.18,"end":154.263,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":145.18,"end":146.805},{"text":"Remember... air goes in, burps come out!","start":147.273,"end":149.998},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":150.495,"end":153.086},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":153.582,"end":154.213}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep23":{"id":"ep23","title":"Why does popcorn pop?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":190.115,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":12.412,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"Why does popcorn pop?","start":10.93,"end":12.362}]},"s1":{"start":13.512,"end":21.177,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"It's movie night!","start":13.512,"end":14.426},{"text":"A grown-up is making popcorn, in a pan with a lid...","start":14.89,"end":18.607},{"text":"and I'm watching from a safe spot.","start":19.211,"end":21.127}]},"s2":{"start":22.077,"end":28.782,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Pop!","start":22.077,"end":22.418},{"text":"Pop, pop, pop!","start":22.921,"end":23.613},{"text":"The popcorn jumps around inside the pan...","start":24.077,"end":26.7},{"text":"and the lid keeps it in.","start":27.335,"end":28.732}]},"s3":{"start":29.882,"end":37.062,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Now it's cooled down.","start":29.882,"end":31.122},{"text":"I'll toss one up...","start":31.518,"end":32.457},{"text":"and catch it!","start":33.08,"end":33.807},{"text":"Oops!","start":34.74,"end":35.274},{"text":"It bonked me on the head!","start":35.773,"end":37.012}]},"s4":{"start":38.062,"end":43.061,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"One more try...","start":38.062,"end":38.909},{"text":"Got it!","start":39.535,"end":40.094},{"text":"Yummy!","start":40.538,"end":41.13},{"text":"Pop-tastic!","start":42.22,"end":43.011}]},"guess":{"start":44.061,"end":50.624,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"But why do they jump?","start":44.061,"end":45.251},{"text":"Are there tiny jumping beans inside...","start":45.8,"end":47.997},{"text":"bouncing on tiny trampolines?","start":48.61,"end":50.574}]},"look":{"start":51.724,"end":57.319,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Hmm, maybe not!","start":51.724,"end":53.066},{"text":"Let's look inside a popcorn seed...","start":53.56,"end":55.531},{"text":"It's called a kernel.","start":56.166,"end":57.269}]},"e1":{"start":58.619,"end":65.658,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"A kernel has a hard shell...","start":58.619,"end":60.345},{"text":"soft starch inside, its food store...","start":60.869,"end":63.4},{"text":"and a tiny drop of water.","start":63.98,"end":65.608}]},"e2":{"start":66.658,"end":72.762,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"When the pan gets hot...","start":66.658,"end":67.815},{"text":"the water turns into steam.","start":68.426,"end":70.175},{"text":"Steam needs much more room than water!","start":70.591,"end":72.712}]},"e3":{"start":73.762,"end":84.423,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"The steam pushes on the shell...","start":73.762,"end":75.389},{"text":"but the shell is strong.","start":75.932,"end":77.298},{"text":"The push gets bigger...","start":77.714,"end":79.203},{"text":"and bigger, and bigger!","start":79.778,"end":81.302},{"text":"Scientists call this push... pressure.","start":81.783,"end":84.373}]},"e4":{"start":85.423,"end":97.641,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"When it's very hot, much hotter than boiling water...","start":85.423,"end":88.585},{"text":"the shell cracks...","start":89.125,"end":90.273},{"text":"and the steam whooshes out.","start":90.881,"end":92.87},{"text":"Pop!","start":93.308,"end":93.649},{"text":"The soft starch puffs out into white fluff, and sets.","start":94.112,"end":97.591}]},"e5":{"start":98.641,"end":103.829,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The puff pushes on the pan like a tiny leg...","start":98.641,"end":100.911},{"text":"and boing!","start":101.523,"end":102.211},{"text":"The popcorn jumps!","start":102.646,"end":103.779}]},"test":{"start":105.129,"end":112.183,"hold":0.5,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists test it!","start":105.129,"end":106.571},{"text":"Here's a kernel with a crack in its shell.","start":107.032,"end":109.149},{"text":"Let's heat it...","start":109.592,"end":110.304},{"text":"next to a good one.","start":110.927,"end":112.133}]},"t1":{"start":113.183,"end":122.32,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The good one pops!","start":113.183,"end":114.457},{"text":"The cracked one just hisses...","start":114.949,"end":116.728},{"text":"the steam leaks out of the crack, so the push never gets big.","start":117.271,"end":121.063},{"text":"No pop!","start":121.482,"end":122.27}]},"real":{"start":123.62,"end":137.5,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Wow fact!","start":123.62,"end":125.484},{"text":"The oldest popcorn found so far...","start":125.919,"end":127.701},{"text":"is about six thousand, seven hundred years old!","start":128.284,"end":132.198},{"text":"People in Peru were popping corn...","start":133.362,"end":135.604},{"text":"long, long ago!","start":136.131,"end":137.45}]},"safe":{"start":138.6,"end":143.985,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Popping needs a very hot pan...","start":138.6,"end":140.687},{"text":"so popping popcorn is a job for grown-ups.","start":141.244,"end":143.935}]},"sayit":{"start":145.285,"end":152.808,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":145.285,"end":146.349},{"text":"Steam inside makes it pop!","start":146.946,"end":148.538},{"text":"Steam inside...","start":149.898,"end":151.035},{"text":"makes it pop!","start":151.767,"end":152.758}]},"try1":{"start":154.708,"end":159.936,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":154.708,"end":155.777},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for a paper bag...","start":156.2,"end":158.039},{"text":"never a plastic one!","start":158.65,"end":159.886}]},"try2":{"start":160.836,"end":175.806,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Blow it full of air, a few big breaths...","start":160.836,"end":163.449},{"text":"then rest.","start":164.039,"end":165.004},{"text":"Twist it shut...","start":165.501,"end":166.483},{"text":"hold it away from faces...","start":167.108,"end":168.697},{"text":"and clap it!","start":169.313,"end":169.946},{"text":"Pop!","start":170.363,"end":170.704},{"text":"What burst the bag?","start":171.482,"end":172.68},{"text":"The squeezed air...","start":173.265,"end":174.432},{"text":"pushing out!","start":175.014,"end":175.756}]},"bye":{"start":177.906,"end":186.715,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":177.906,"end":179.531},{"text":"Remember... steam inside makes it pop!","start":179.949,"end":182.522},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":182.962,"end":185.554},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":186.034,"end":186.665}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep24":{"id":"ep24","title":"How does a caterpillar become a butterfly?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":194.777,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":13.356,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"How does a caterpillar become a butterfly?","start":10.93,"end":13.306}]},"s1":{"start":14.456,"end":22.486,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"I'm in the garden.","start":14.456,"end":15.425},{"text":"Look!","start":15.849,"end":16.241},{"text":"Someone has been nibbling this leaf...","start":16.72,"end":18.311},{"text":"Munch, munch, munch!","start":18.867,"end":20.059},{"text":"It's a hungry little caterpillar!","start":20.52,"end":22.436}]},"s2":{"start":23.286,"end":29.61,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"And over there...","start":23.286,"end":24.296},{"text":"a butterfly!","start":24.93,"end":25.772},{"text":"A butterfly was once a caterpillar.","start":26.236,"end":28.408},{"text":"But how?","start":28.868,"end":29.56}]},"guess":{"start":30.41,"end":38.189,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Maybe the caterpillar put on a butterfly costume?","start":30.41,"end":33.5},{"text":"Flap, flap...","start":34.067,"end":35.059},{"text":"Whoa!","start":35.682,"end":36.173},{"text":"Hmm... maybe not!","start":36.606,"end":38.139}]},"look":{"start":39.289,"end":42.102,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's look closer...","start":39.289,"end":40.404},{"text":"right from the start.","start":41.029,"end":42.052}]},"e1":{"start":43.202,"end":53.851,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"A monarch butterfly lays a tiny egg on a leaf.","start":43.202,"end":46.218},{"text":"Out wriggles a tiny caterpillar.","start":46.602,"end":48.66},{"text":"And its first breakfast is...","start":49.103,"end":50.818},{"text":"its own eggshell!","start":51.391,"end":52.729},{"text":"Crunch!","start":53.21,"end":53.801}]},"e2":{"start":54.651,"end":63.881,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Then it eats leaves, all day long.","start":54.651,"end":57.279},{"text":"Munch, munch, munch!","start":57.731,"end":58.922},{"text":"It grows...","start":59.426,"end":60.427},{"text":"and grows.","start":61.05,"end":62.133},{"text":"It's always hungry!","start":62.596,"end":63.831}]},"e3":{"start":64.681,"end":75.082,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"But its skin can't grow with it.","start":64.681,"end":66.421},{"text":"When it gets too tight...","start":66.8,"end":67.792},{"text":"the caterpillar wriggles out of its old skin,","start":68.372,"end":71.005},{"text":"like a too-tight sweater!","start":71.165,"end":72.73},{"text":"Again, and again.","start":73.356,"end":75.032}]},"wow":{"start":75.982,"end":88.575,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Wow fact!","start":75.982,"end":77.846},{"text":"In about two weeks...","start":78.331,"end":79.473},{"text":"a monarch caterpillar gets about two thousand times heavier.","start":79.997,"end":83.448},{"text":"If a baby grew like that...","start":83.921,"end":85.393},{"text":"it would be as heavy as a big elephant!","start":86.002,"end":88.525}]},"e4":{"start":89.675,"end":96.985,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"When it's big enough, it hangs upside down...","start":89.675,"end":92.167},{"text":"and sheds its skin one last time.","start":92.71,"end":94.73},{"text":"Underneath is a chrysalis!","start":95.145,"end":96.935}]},"e5":{"start":97.785,"end":107.807,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Inside the chrysalis...","start":97.785,"end":99.177},{"text":"its body breaks down...","start":99.717,"end":101.034},{"text":"and is built again, into a butterfly!","start":101.642,"end":104.377},{"text":"This big change is called... metamorphosis.","start":104.815,"end":107.757}]},"silly":{"start":108.607,"end":118.418,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Meta, mor, pho, sis!","start":108.607,"end":110.549},{"text":"Can you say that ten times fast?","start":111.034,"end":112.819},{"text":"Metamorphosis, metamorphosis, metamorpho...","start":113.387,"end":117.159},{"text":"Phew!","start":117.977,"end":118.368}]},"e6":{"start":119.318,"end":129.29,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Then... out comes the butterfly!","start":119.318,"end":121.71},{"text":"Its wings are wet and crumpled.","start":122.171,"end":124.198},{"text":"It pumps them up...","start":124.641,"end":125.624},{"text":"lets them dry...","start":126.247,"end":127.534},{"text":"and flies away!","start":128.057,"end":129.24}]},"e7":{"start":130.09,"end":135.814,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Later, it lays tiny eggs on a leaf...","start":130.09,"end":132.352},{"text":"and it all starts again.","start":132.973,"end":134.351},{"text":"Round and round!","start":134.781,"end":135.764}]},"test":{"start":136.914,"end":148.249,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"How do scientists know?","start":136.914,"end":138.524},{"text":"They watch!","start":139.091,"end":140.293},{"text":"They look after caterpillars every day...","start":140.755,"end":142.647},{"text":"and see a caterpillar turn into a chrysalis...","start":143.233,"end":146.237},{"text":"then a butterfly.","start":146.764,"end":148.199}]},"sayit":{"start":149.349,"end":160.061,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":149.349,"end":150.413},{"text":"Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly!","start":150.969,"end":154.611},{"text":"Egg... caterpillar... chrysalis... butterfly!","start":155.974,"end":160.011}]},"try1":{"start":161.961,"end":166.806,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":161.961,"end":163.03},{"text":"Go on a leaf hunt...","start":163.804,"end":164.946},{"text":"with a grown-up.","start":165.526,"end":166.756}]},"try2":{"start":167.706,"end":179.317,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Ask a grown-up to gently turn over some leaves...","start":167.706,"end":170.177},{"text":"Look for tiny eggs...","start":170.711,"end":172.083},{"text":"and nibbled holes.","start":172.693,"end":174.251},{"text":"Don't touch...","start":174.688,"end":175.585},{"text":"and wash your hands after!","start":176.123,"end":177.606},{"text":"Can you find a nibbler?","start":178.051,"end":179.267}]},"bye":{"start":181.017,"end":191.377,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":181.017,"end":182.642},{"text":"Remember... egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly!","start":183.11,"end":187.112},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":187.609,"end":190.201},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":190.697,"end":191.327}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"},"ep25":{"id":"ep25","title":"How did John Snow find which pump made people sick?","titleEnd":2.8,"duration":182.086,"lines":{"hello":{"start":3.05,"end":8.566,"hold":0.3,"chunks":[{"text":"Hello, little scientists!","start":3.05,"end":4.634},{"text":"I'm Curie!","start":5.086,"end":5.757},{"text":"Welcome... to the Little Scientists Club!","start":6.237,"end":8.516}]},"q":{"start":9.366,"end":14.128,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Today's big question...","start":9.366,"end":10.358},{"text":"How did John Snow find which pump, made people sick?","start":10.93,"end":14.078}]},"back":{"start":15.228,"end":24.031,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Last time...","start":15.228,"end":16.217},{"text":"Biscuit and I rode the Time Bubble, back to eighteen fifty-four...","start":16.803,"end":20.034},{"text":"and saw John Snow compare two water companies.","start":20.639,"end":23.981}]},"hat":{"start":24.931,"end":32.534,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Whoosh!","start":24.931,"end":25.523},{"text":"Now we're off to Soho, in London, for another clue!","start":26.026,"end":29.267},{"text":"Oops!","start":29.732,"end":30.266},{"text":"My detective hat is a bit big!","start":30.769,"end":32.484}]},"s1":{"start":33.634,"end":42.672,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"At the end of summer, in eighteen fifty-four...","start":33.634,"end":36.163},{"text":"in a busy part of London called Soho...","start":36.711,"end":38.903},{"text":"lots of people became very ill, all at once.","start":39.526,"end":42.622}]},"s2":{"start":43.572,"end":49.051,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Back then, many people got their water from pumps, in the street.","start":43.572,"end":47.159},{"text":"Pump, pump, pump!","start":47.612,"end":49.001}]},"look":{"start":49.951,"end":56.238,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Which pump was making people ill?","start":49.951,"end":51.863},{"text":"John Snow looked for clues.","start":52.412,"end":54.452},{"text":"Let's look closer!","start":54.896,"end":56.188}]},"e1":{"start":57.538,"end":63.242,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"He found out where the people with cholera lived...","start":57.538,"end":59.915},{"text":"and put a dot on a map, for each home.","start":60.538,"end":63.192}]},"e2":{"start":64.342,"end":68.378,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Most of the dots crowded close to one pump...","start":64.342,"end":66.722},{"text":"on Broad Street.","start":67.245,"end":68.328}]},"sniff":{"start":69.478,"end":76.362,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's play connect the dots!","start":69.478,"end":70.996},{"text":"Biscuit, follow the dots...","start":71.481,"end":73.263},{"text":"Sniff, sniff...","start":73.787,"end":74.929},{"text":"Woof, woof!","start":75.525,"end":76.312}]},"check":{"start":77.462,"end":83.036,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Scientists check the clues!","start":77.462,"end":79.454},{"text":"Most of the people...","start":79.884,"end":80.82},{"text":"had drunk water from that one pump.","start":81.345,"end":82.986}]},"e5":{"start":84.336,"end":90.538,"hold":0.6,"chunks":[{"text":"So he told the people in charge...","start":84.336,"end":86.088},{"text":"and they took the handle off the pump...","start":86.628,"end":88.442},{"text":"so nobody could use it.","start":89.05,"end":90.488}]},"nope":{"start":91.638,"end":97.26,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Biscuit wants a drink.","start":91.638,"end":92.856},{"text":"But... nothing comes out!","start":93.299,"end":95.012},{"text":"No handle, no water!","start":95.448,"end":97.21}]},"e6":{"start":98.56,"end":103.991,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"The illness was already slowing down.","start":98.56,"end":100.389},{"text":"But now, that pump could not make anyone else ill.","start":100.81,"end":103.941}]},"e7":{"start":105.291,"end":114.95,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Later, people found that dirty water, from a leaky toilet pit next to the pump...","start":105.291,"end":110.255},{"text":"could seep into it.","start":110.876,"end":112.017},{"text":"Biscuit sniffs it...","start":112.447,"end":113.63},{"text":"pee-yew!","start":114.158,"end":114.9}]},"case":{"start":116.25,"end":126.474,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Clue one...","start":116.25,"end":117.15},{"text":"the dots.","start":117.697,"end":118.498},{"text":"Clue two...","start":118.96,"end":119.601},{"text":"the water they drank.","start":120.187,"end":121.325},{"text":"And later, clue three...","start":121.743,"end":123.264},{"text":"the leaky pit.","start":123.828,"end":125.008},{"text":"Mystery solved!","start":125.442,"end":126.424}]},"real":{"start":127.974,"end":134.94,"hold":1,"chunks":[{"text":"Today, John Snow is called...","start":127.974,"end":129.516},{"text":"the father of epidemiology.","start":130.072,"end":131.663},{"text":"The science of finding out what makes people ill.","start":132.126,"end":134.89}]},"home":{"start":136.44,"end":141.951,"hold":0.8,"chunks":[{"text":"Time to go home!","start":136.44,"end":137.232},{"text":"Into the Time Bubble... whoosh!","start":137.705,"end":139.777},{"text":"Biscuit is fast asleep.","start":140.237,"end":141.901}]},"sayit":{"start":143.251,"end":151.021,"hold":1.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Say it with me...","start":143.251,"end":144.315},{"text":"Follow the clues to find the cause!","start":144.848,"end":146.619},{"text":"Follow the clues...","start":148.003,"end":149.044},{"text":"to find the cause!","start":149.69,"end":150.971}]},"try1":{"start":152.921,"end":159.104,"hold":0.4,"chunks":[{"text":"Let's try it at home!","start":152.921,"end":153.99},{"text":"Ask a grown-up for paper and a pencil...","start":154.458,"end":156.339},{"text":"and draw a map of your kitchen floor.","start":156.965,"end":159.054}]},"try2":{"start":160.004,"end":167.412,"hold":1.6,"chunks":[{"text":"After snack time, put a dot on your map for every crumb.","start":160.004,"end":163.899},{"text":"Where are the most dots?","start":164.281,"end":165.723},{"text":"What is near them?","start":166.297,"end":167.362}]},"bye":{"start":169.512,"end":178.686,"hold":1.2,"chunks":[{"text":"Great job, little scientist!","start":169.512,"end":171.137},{"text":"Remember... follow the clues to find the cause!","start":171.57,"end":174.503},{"text":"See you next time, at the Little Scientists Club!","start":174.961,"end":177.553},{"text":"Bye-bye!","start":178.005,"end":178.636}]}},"voice":"chirp:achernar"}},"meta":{"ep1":{"title":"Why does ice cream melt?","hint":"Drag the sun close to the ice cream to melt it. Tap the freezer to make it hard again.","cues":{"melt":"The sun is warm! Heat makes the ice cream melt.","cold":"Brrr! The freezer is cold. The ice cream is hard again."}},"ep2":{"title":"Why does a ball always come down?","hint":"Drag the ball and let go to throw it. Try to make it float away — you can't! Tap the big and small balls to drop them together.","cues":{"down":"Up it goes... and down it comes. Gravity pulls it down every time!","together":"Big ball and small ball land together!"}},"ep3":{"title":"Where does rain come from?","hint":"Drag the sun down to warm the sea and lift the water up. When the cloud gets heavy, tap it to make rain!","cues":{"warm":"The sun warms the sea. Tiny bits of water float up!","heavy":"The cloud is heavy now. Tap it!"}},"ep4":{"title":"Why do I have a shadow?","hint":"Drag the sun across the sky and watch Curie's shadow stretch and shrink. Drag Curie too!","cues":{"low":"The sun is low, so the shadow is long!","high":"The sun is high, so the shadow is short!"}},"ep5":{"title":"Why do boats float?","hint":"Guess first, then drag each thing into the pond. Does it sink or float? Tap the clay to change its shape.","cues":{"sink":"Down it goes! It sinks.","float":"It stays on top! The water pushes it up."}},"ep6":{"title":"What makes things happen?","hint":"Drag the pieces into the pot: soil, seed, water and sunshine. When the cause pie is full, wait and watch. Drag a piece back out to take it away, and try teddy too!","cues":{"wait":"The pie is full! Now we wait... causes take time.","grow":"All the pieces together... and the flower grows!","away":"Take one piece away... and it doesn't happen!","teddy":"Teddy is just there. Teddy is not a cause!","missing":"Something is missing, so the flower can't grow yet."}},"ep7":{"title":"Why do magnets stick?","hint":"Drag the magnet near each thing. Iron things jump to the magnet. Which ones stick?","cues":{"iron":"It sticks! That one has iron in it.","nope":"No pull. That one has no iron in it.","foil":"Foil is a metal, but it has no iron. No pull!","pull":"Red and blue pull together. Click!","push":"Red and red push apart! The same ends push each other away."},"tasks":[{"title":"Magnet test","hint":"Drag the magnet near each thing. Iron things jump to the magnet. Which ones stick?"},{"title":"Push or pull","hint":"Drag the magnet towards the other one. Tap it to turn it around. Which ends pull, and which push?"}]},"ep8":{"title":"How do we hear sounds?","hint":"Tap a string to pluck it. Long strings make low sounds and short strings make high sounds.","cues":{"low":"A long string wiggles more slowly. A low sound!","high":"A short string wiggles faster. A high sound!","quiet":"A small tap makes small wiggles: a quiet sound.","loud":"A big bang makes big wiggles: a loud sound! Look at the rice jump!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Pluck the strings","hint":"Tap a string to pluck it. Long strings make low sounds and short strings make high sounds."},{"title":"Tap the drum","hint":"Pull the drumstick back and let go. A small pull taps softly; a big pull bangs hard. Watch the rice!"}]},"ep9":{"title":"Why is it dark at night?","hint":"Drag around the Earth to spin it. Put Curie's house in the sunshine, then in the dark.","cues":{"day":"Curie's house faces the sun. It's daytime!","night":"Curie's house turned away from the sun. It's night!","sunrise":"The sun comes up. Good morning!","sunset":"The sun goes down. Good night!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Spin the Earth","hint":"Drag around the Earth to spin it. Put Curie's house in the sunshine, then in the dark."},{"title":"Sunrise, sunset","hint":"Spin the little Earth. Watch the sky over Curie's garden: the sun comes up, then goes down!"}]},"ep10":{"title":"Where does the sugar go?","hint":"Drag the sugar into the glass, then stir with the spoon (round and round). Where does it go? Tap Curie to taste!","cues":{"gone":"The sugar disappeared... or did it?","sweet":"Yum, sweet! The sugar is still there.","dissolves":"It dissolves! The tiny bits spread out in the water.","stays":"It doesn't dissolve. It sinks to the bottom and stays there."},"tasks":[{"title":"Stir the sugar","hint":"Drag the sugar into the glass, then stir with the spoon (round and round). Where does it go? Tap Curie to taste!"},{"title":"Does it dissolve?","hint":"Drag each jar into a glass. Which ones dissolve? Which ones sink and stay?"}]},"ep11":{"title":"Why do rolling things stop?","hint":"Pick a floor: ice, wood, carpet or grass. Then tap the car to let it go! Where does it stop?","cues":{"far":"Slippery ice hardly rubs at all. The car rolls a long, long way!","middle":"Smooth wood rubs a little. The car rolls quite far.","short":"Bumpy floors rub a lot. The car stops quickly!","slide":"On ice, a little push... and it slides and slides!","hard":"Sand rubs a lot. It's hard to push, and it stops right away!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Ramp race","hint":"Pick a floor: ice, wood, carpet or grass. Then tap the car to let it go! Where does it stop?"},{"title":"Push the box","hint":"Push the box along with your finger. On ice it slides easily; on sand it's hard work!"}]},"ep17":{"title":"Where do rainbows come from?","hint":"Drag the sun across the sky. When is there a rainbow over the rain?","cues":{"behind":"The sun is behind me... a rainbow!","front":"Ouch, too bright! Facing the sun, no rainbow.","high":"The sun is too high! The rainbow hides below the ground.","split":"All the colours were hiding in the white light!","drop":"A raindrop does it too!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Make a rainbow","hint":"Drag the sun across the sky. When is there a rainbow over the rain?"},{"title":"Split the light","hint":"Drag the torch up and down to shine on the prism. Then tap the drop!"}]},"ep18":{"title":"Why does the Moon change shape?","hint":"Drag the Moon around the Earth. How does it look from the Earth?","cues":{"new":"A new Moon! Its dark half faces us.","half":"Half of the Moon looks bright!","full":"A full Moon! All of its bright half faces us.","grow":"Night by night, the bright part grows!","shrink":"Now the bright part shrinks, night by night.","again":"About twenty-nine and a half days... and it starts again!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Moon trip","hint":"Drag the Moon around the Earth. How does it look from the Earth?"},{"title":"Night by night","hint":"Drag the dot along, one night at a time. Watch the Moon grow and shrink!"}]},"ep19":{"title":"How do we know what made something happen?","hint":"Change one thing in the what-if race: the socks, or the road. Then tap Go! Which change makes a difference?","cues":{"twin":"Everything the same... so the same time!","same":"Plain socks, same race... same time! The socks make no difference.","hill":"Uphill, the what-if me is slower. The hill makes a difference!","two":"Two things changed! Now we cannot tell which one made the difference.","unfair":"Not fair! All the fast runners are on the spotty team.","fair":"Picked from a hat, the teams are alike... and about as fast!","again":"Picked fairly! But by chance, these teams came out a bit different. With lots more runners, the hat makes teams more alike."},"tasks":[{"title":"What if?","hint":"Change one thing in the what-if race: the socks, or the road. Then tap Go! Which change makes a difference?"},{"title":"Pick from a hat","hint":"Make two teams. Put all the fast runners on one team, or pick from a hat. Which way is fair?"}]},"ep20":{"title":"How did a doctor test what was making people sick?","hint":"Tap a house to switch it between the dirty-water pipe and the cleaner-water pipe. Then tap \"Wait a month\". Which homes have more cholera?","cues":{"more":"Dirty-water homes got cholera far more often!","same":"Every home has the dirty water, so there is nothing to compare. Give some homes the cleaner pipe!","clean":"All the homes have cleaner water, and hardly anyone got ill. But now there is nothing to compare!","fair":"Fair! Alike in every way, except the water.","unfair":"Not fair! Their size and their water are both different.","samew":"Same water in both groups. That cannot test the water!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Two water pipes","hint":"Tap a house to switch it between the dirty-water pipe and the cleaner-water pipe. Then tap \"Wait a month\". Which homes have more cholera?"},{"title":"Fair or not fair?","hint":"Which way of comparing two groups of homes is a fair test of the water? Tap it."}]},"ep21":{"title":"Why can't we take big bottles or sharp things on a plane?","hint":"Drag things into your cabin bag, then tap \"Scan my bag\". What does the officer say?","cues":{"sharp":"Scissors! Sharp things go in the big suitcase, under the plane.","big":"That bottle is too big for the cabin. Only small bottles!","clear":"All clear! Have a lovely flight!","pass":"Pale! X-rays pass through soft things.","block":"Dark! Metal blocks the X-rays.","alike":"Water, juice and shampoo all look the same to the scanner!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Pack my bag","hint":"Drag things into your cabin bag, then tap \"Scan my bag\". What does the officer say?"},{"title":"X-ray eyes","hint":"Tap each thing to see it the way the X-ray scanner does. Which look pale, which look dark, and which look the same?"}]},"ep22":{"title":"Why do we burp?","hint":"Drag the sandwich, the water and the fizzy drink to the mouth. Watch the air bubbles gather. What happens when the tummy is full of gas?","cues":{"bubbles":"Gulp! Some little air bubbles went down too.","fizz":"Fizzy drinks fill the tummy with gas bubbles!","burp":"Oops! Out comes a burp. Excuse me!","flat":"Plain water has no fizz, so its balloon stays flat.","puff":"Fizzy water! Its gas puffs up the balloon a little.","full":"See? The fizz is a gas. In a tummy, it comes out as a burp."},"tasks":[{"title":"Fill the tummy","hint":"Drag the sandwich, the water and the fizzy drink to the mouth. Watch the air bubbles gather. What happens when the tummy is full of gas?"},{"title":"Balloon on a bottle","hint":"Tap each bottle to swirl it. Which balloon puffs up a little: the plain water or the fizzy water?"}]},"ep23":{"title":"Why does popcorn pop?","hint":"Drag the red HEAT knob up. Watch the water turn to steam and the push grow. What happens at about 180 °C?","cues":{"steam":"The water is turning into steam!","pop":"Pop! The steam burst the shell. Pop-tastic!","yes":"Pop! The good kernel kept its steam inside.","leak":"Hiss! The steam leaked out of the crack. No pop.","dry":"Nothing! It dried out, so there is too little water to make enough steam. No pop."},"tasks":[{"title":"Heat a kernel","hint":"Drag the red HEAT knob up. Watch the water turn to steam and the push grow. What happens at about 180 °C?"},{"title":"Which will pop?","hint":"Tap each kernel to heat it: a good one, one with a cracked shell, and a dried-out one. Which one pops?"}]},"ep24":{"title":"How does a caterpillar become a butterfly?","hint":"Drag leaves to the hungry caterpillar. Watch it grow, wriggle out of its old skin... and then what happens?","cues":{"munch":"Munch, munch! The caterpillar eats, and grows bigger.","shed":"Too tight! It wriggles out of its old skin.","fly":"A chrysalis... and now a butterfly! That is metamorphosis!","order":"Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly! You did it!","round":"And the butterfly lays new eggs. Round and round it goes!","hmm":"Not quite! What comes first? An egg!"},"tasks":[{"title":"Feed the caterpillar","hint":"Drag leaves to the hungry caterpillar. Watch it grow, wriggle out of its old skin... and then what happens?"},{"title":"Life cycle circle","hint":"Drag the four cards into the circle, in order from number 1. What comes first?"}]},"ep25":{"title":"How did John Snow find which pump made people sick?","hint":"Tap \"Find more homes\" to mark where cholera struck. Where do the dots crowd together? Tap the pump you think made people ill.","cues":{"clue":"Look where the dots crowd together. Which pump is close to them?","pump":"Yes! Most dots are close to the Broad Street pump. Off comes the handle!","other":"Only a few dots are near this pump. Look where the dots crowd together!","peak":"This is the day the most people became very ill.","slow":"Look! The illness is already slowing down.","off":"Handle off! Now that pump cannot make anyone else ill."},"tasks":[{"title":"Find the pump","hint":"Tap \"Find more homes\" to mark where cholera struck. Where do the dots crowd together? Tap the pump you think made people ill."},{"title":"Day by day","hint":"Tap \"Next day\" to add each day's count of people who became very ill. You can tap \"Take the handle off\" whenever you like. When did the illness start slowing down?"}]}},"videos":{"ep1":"https://youtu.be/X-jBQuLV9Jk","ep2":"https://youtu.be/0GUWsj-WB84","ep3":"https://youtu.be/FMA3AsAC_9M","ep4":"https://youtu.be/1FCzzUReXo4","ep5":"https://youtu.be/0fPIiTQ2eMw","ep6":"https://youtu.be/xREaUbLIwcA","ep7":"https://youtu.be/uciCm4EvLqo","ep8":"https://youtu.be/VJIvcYfYEe8","ep9":"https://youtu.be/LkSeCHwiIDs","ep10":"https://youtu.be/YGpuX1xqKQc","ep11":"https://youtu.be/lWossmCt6V8","ep17":"https://youtu.be/gxGyiVvFtb8","ep19":"https://youtu.be/bmoZPf17dCE","ep20":"https://youtu.be/Ax7cvERpsts","ep18":"https://youtu.be/Mr9AyIHbFsQ","ep21":"https://youtu.be/okDmAioCYBI","ep22":"https://youtu.be/0rHWYhN86fo","ep23":"https://youtu.be/OEZm7kRKsgA","ep24":"https://youtu.be/rZ8dBQ3qZQU","ep25":"https://youtu.be/7ESGenSNBKY"},"channel":"https://www.youtube.com/@LittleScientistsClubCurie","content":{"ep1":{"idea":"Heat makes things melt!","explain":"Ice cream is made of tiny bits. When it is cold they hold on to each other tightly, so the ice cream is hard: a solid. Warmth from the sun or a hand gives the bits energy, they wiggle apart and slide past each other, and the ice cream becomes a liquid. In the freezer the bits slow down and grab hold again.","play":"drag the sun","steps":["Ask a grown-up for two ice cubes and two small plates.","Put one plate in the sunshine and one in the shade.","Guess which cube will melt first, then check every few minutes.","Talk about it: what gave the ice cube heat?"],"quiz":[["What makes ice cream melt?",["Heat","Cold","Wind"],0],["Cold, hard ice cream is a...",["Solid","Liquid","Cloud"],0],["Where does melted ice cream go to get hard again?",["The freezer","The sun","The bath"],0]],"color":"#FF9BD2"},"ep2":{"idea":"Gravity pulls things down!","explain":"The Earth is enormous, and it pulls everything towards itself. We call that pull gravity. It is why a thrown ball always comes back down, why rain falls, and why our feet stay on the ground. Gravity makes big and small things fall together, so a big ball and a small ball dropped at the same time land together.","play":"throw the ball","steps":["Hold a ball in one hand and a toy car or a wooden block in the other, at the same height.","Count to three and let go of both at exactly the same time.","Watch closely: which one lands first? Try it again to be sure.","Now throw the ball up gently. Which way does it always go?"],"quiz":[["What pulls the ball back down?",["Gravity","The wind","A string"],0],["A big ball and a small ball dropped together land...",["Together","Big one first","Small one first"],0],["Gravity keeps your feet...",["On the ground","In the sky","In the sea"],0]],"color":"#9B6BFF"},"ep3":{"idea":"The sun lifts the water up, and the rain brings it down!","explain":"The sun warms the sea, and tiny bits of water float up into the air as invisible water vapour. High in the sky it is cold, so the bits huddle into droplets, and millions of droplets make a cloud. The droplets bump and join until they are too heavy to float, then they fall as rain, which runs back to the sea. Round and round: the water cycle.","play":"make it rain","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a glass or cup of very cold water with ice cubes.","Leave it on the table and wait a few minutes.","Look at the outside of the glass. Where did the little drops come from?","Breathe on a cold window or mirror and watch a tiny cloud appear."],"quiz":[["What lifts water up into the sky?",["The warm sun","The moon","A fan"],0],["Millions of tiny droplets make a...",["Cloud","Rock","Tree"],0],["Rain falls when the drops get too...",["Heavy","Light","Warm"],0]],"color":"#4EA8FF"},"ep4":{"idea":"A shadow is where the light can't go!","explain":"Light travels in straight lines. When it hits your body it stops, because it cannot go through you, so the ground or wall behind you gets no light. That dark patch is your shadow. When the sun is low in the morning or evening, shadows stretch long; at noon, when the sun is high, they are short.","play":"stretch a shadow","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a torch and make the room dark. Never shine it in anyone's eyes.","Shine the torch on a wall and put your hand in the light.","Make a dog, a bird or a rabbit with your hands.","Move your hand closer to the torch. Does the shadow get bigger or smaller?"],"quiz":[["A shadow is where...",["Light can't go","Light is brightest","Water is"],0],["When the sun is low, your shadow is...",["Long","Short","Gone"],0],["Light travels in...",["Straight lines","Circles","Zigzags"],0]],"color":"#FFB03B"},"ep5":{"idea":"Water pushes up!","explain":"When something sits in water it pushes some water out of the way, and the water pushes back up. A small, heavy stone pushes only a little water aside, so the push is too small and it sinks. A wide, hollow boat full of air pushes a lot of water aside, so the water pushes up hard enough to hold it. The same clay sinks as a ball and floats as a boat.","play":"sort things into the pond","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a bowl of water.","Collect a spoon, a coin, a cork, a plastic lid and a leaf.","Guess first: will it sink or float? Then drop it in.","Roll some clay or foil into a ball, then shape it into a boat. Test both!"],"quiz":[["Water pushes...",["Up","Down","Sideways"],0],["A small stone sinks because it pushes away only a little...",["Water","Air","Sand"],0],["The same clay floats when it is shaped like a...",["Boat","Ball","Cube"],0]],"color":"#5BC26B"},"ep6":{"idea":"Lots of pieces make it happen!","explain":"Things rarely have just one cause. A flower needs soil, a seed, water and sunshine all together, and it takes time. Epidemiologists draw this as a causal pie (Kenneth Rothman's sufficient-component cause model): each slice is a component cause, and the thing happens only when the whole pie is complete. The pieces need not act at the same time: the flower shows once the last piece has done its work (Rothman calls that gap the induction period). Because every slice is needed, taking any one away stops that pie, which is how prevention works: wash the germs off your hands and that tummy ache cannot happen. The same thing can also happen through a different pie (a flower can get its water from rain instead of a watering can), so removing one piece prevents the cases that needed it, not every case. To find out whether something really is a cause, scientists test it by taking it away and seeing whether the result changes. Teddy sat beside the pot all week, but the flower grew just the same without him: being there is not the same as being a cause.","play":"fill Curie's cause pie","steps":["Ask a grown-up for two clear cups, some cotton wool and two dried bean seeds.","Put cotton wool and a bean in each cup, and stand both cups on a sunny windowsill.","Water one cup a little every day so the wool stays damp. Keep the other cup dry.","Check every day for a week. Which bean grows? Which piece of the pie was missing?"],"quiz":[["What does a flower need to grow?",["All the pieces together","Just a seed","A teddy"],0],["Take one piece away and the flower...",["Does not grow","Grows faster","Turns blue"],0],["Teddy sat next to the pot every day. Is teddy a cause?",["No, teddy was just there","Yes, teddy made it grow","Only on Sundays"],0]],"color":"#E7C27A"},"ep7":{"idea":"Magnets pull iron!","explain":"A magnet pulls on things that contain iron, such as steel paper clips, iron nails, steel bottle caps and the steel door of many fridges (steel is mostly iron). Things without iron, such as wood, plastic, paper and leaves, are not pulled, and neither are most other metals: aluminium foil, most drink cans, copper and brass are not magnetic. The iron in foods such as spinach or dates is a tiny amount locked inside other substances, so a magnet does not pull them. Coins, keys and spoons vary, which makes them unreliable tests, and some stainless steel does not hold a magnet at all, so a steel paper clip is the surest first test. (Nickel and cobalt are magnetic too, but iron and steel are the ones children meet.) The pull works across a gap, even through paper, but we cannot see it. Every magnet has two ends, called poles (north and south, shown here as red and blue): opposite poles pull together and like poles push apart. Scientists find out which materials are magnetic by testing them one at a time, just as children do in the home experiment. Safety: use a large fridge magnet, and keep small, strong magnets away from young children because they are dangerous if swallowed.","play":"test things with a magnet","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a large fridge magnet. Magnets never go in your mouth.","Collect a paper clip, a pencil, a leaf and a piece of kitchen foil.","Guess first: will the magnet pull it? Then touch each thing with the flat back of the magnet (the side that sticks to the fridge) and sort them into two piles: pulled and not pulled.","Which things did the magnet pull? Talk about what they have in common. Then try the fridge door!"],"quiz":[["What does a magnet pull?",["Paper","Things with iron in them","Wood"],1],["Kitchen foil is shiny metal. Does a magnet pull it?",["Yes, every metal sticks","Only when it is cold","No, it has no iron"],2],["Two magnets, red end to red end. What happens?",["They push apart","They pull together","They melt"],0]],"color":"#FF5C5C"},"ep8":{"idea":"Sounds are wiggles!","explain":"Every sound starts with a vibration: something moving back and forth very quickly, such as a plucked string, a drum skin or the vocal cords in your throat. The vibrating object pushes on the air next to it, and each little bit of air passes the push on to the next bit, so a sound wave travels through the air to your ear. The air itself does not travel from the string to your ear; each bit only wiggles a little and passes the push along. In the ear, the wave makes the eardrum vibrate too, and the brain hears a sound. Two separate things change a sound. Bigger vibrations are louder (hit the drum harder and the rice jumps higher), and faster vibrations are higher (a short or tightly stretched string vibrates faster than a long or loose one, so it gives a higher note). Plucking the same string harder makes it louder, not higher. Humming with fingers on the throat lets children feel the vibration that makes their own voice.","play":"pluck strings and bang a drum","steps":["Ask a grown-up to join you. Put your fingers gently on your throat and hum. Can you feel the wiggle?","Ask your grown-up to stretch a rubber band over an open box or a plastic tub.","Pluck the rubber band gently, and keep it away from your face. Watch it wiggle and listen to the sound.","Ask your grown-up to pull the band at the side of the box a little tighter and hold it (not too tight!), then pluck again. Is the sound higher or lower?"],"quiz":[["What makes a sound?",["Something sleeping","Something wiggling","Something cold"],1],["A short string wiggles faster. Its sound is...",["Low","Silent","High"],2],["Bang the drum harder and the sound gets...",["Louder","Quieter","Colder"],0]],"color":"#2EC4B6"},"ep9":{"idea":"The Earth spins!","explain":"The Earth is a huge ball that spins all the time, making one full turn about every 24 hours. The sun does not switch off, go to sleep or travel round us at night: it keeps shining, but it can only light the half of the Earth that faces it, so that half has day and the half turned away is in shadow and has night. As the Earth turns, each place is carried into the sunlit half (morning), through the day, and out into the shadowed half (evening), so one full turn gives one day and one night. That is also why the sun seems to come up, cross the sky and go down, although it is really the ground under our feet that is turning. When it is midday on one side of the Earth it is midnight on the opposite side: midday in India, for example, is night-time in the Americas. In our pictures we look down from above the North Pole, where the Earth turns counter-clockwise. The pictures are not to scale: the sun is far bigger than the Earth and very far away. The torch-and-ball experiment is a small model: the torch is the sun, the ball is the Earth and the sticker is your home.","play":"spin the Earth","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a torch and a ball (an orange works too). Put a small sticker on it: that is your house.","Make the room dark. Your grown-up shines the torch at the ball from a few steps away (never into anyone's eyes), so the whole near half of the ball is lit up.","Turn the ball slowly, like the Earth spinning. Watch your sticker.","When is your house in the light, so it is day? When is it in the dark, so it is night?"],"quiz":[["Why do we have day and night?",["The sun switches off","The Earth spins","The moon hides the sun"],1],["When your side of the Earth faces the sun, it is...",["Day","Night","Bedtime"],0],["When it is day for you, on the other side of the Earth it is...",["Also day","Raining","Night"],2]],"color":"#3B4BA8"},"ep10":{"idea":"Sugar dissolves in water, but it's still there!","explain":"Sugar and salt are made of particles far too small to see, just like the ice cream in Episode 1. When sugar goes into water, the water particles pull the sugar particles away from the crystal, and they spread out evenly between the water particles: the sugar dissolves. We cannot see it any more, but it has not gone: the water tastes sweet, and if salty water is left to dry up, the salt crystals appear again. Dissolving is different from melting. Melting is a solid turning into a liquid when it is heated (Episode 1); dissolving is the particles of a solid spreading out through a liquid. Sugar dissolves even in cold water, just more slowly than in warm water, and stirring speeds it up by bringing fresh water to the crystals. Not everything dissolves: sand and pebbles sink to the bottom and stay there. Only taste with an adult's permission, and never taste the sand water.","play":"dissolve sugar in water","steps":["Ask a grown-up for two clear glasses of water, a spoon of sugar, a spoon of sand (or uncooked rice, if you have no sand) and two clean spoons for stirring.","Put the sugar in one glass and the sand in the other. Stir each glass with its own spoon for a minute or two, until you can't see the sugar. Then wash your hands.","Look closely: which one disappears? Which one sinks to the bottom and stays there?","With your grown-up's OK, taste a tiny sip of the sugar water. Is the sugar still there? How do you know? (Never taste the sand water, or the rice water!)"],"quiz":[["When sugar dissolves in water, the sugar is...",["Gone forever","Still there, in tiny bits","Turned into sand"],1],["Which one does NOT dissolve in water?",["Sand","Salt","Sugar"],0],["How can you tell the sugar is still in the water?",["The water turns green","The glass sings","The water tastes sweet"],2]],"color":"#F7B267"},"ep11":{"idea":"Rubbing slows it down!","explain":"A rolling or sliding object slows down because of friction, the force that acts where two surfaces rub against each other (air resistance adds a little). Rough or soft surfaces such as carpet, grass and sand produce more friction than smooth, hard ones, and ice produces very little, so the same car rolls much further on ice than on carpet. For wheels this is rolling friction: soft floors such as carpet squash under the wheels, and the axles rub a little. In the Try it activities the car and the box slow down steadily: the rougher the surface, the stronger the pull back, so the sooner they stop. Friction is also useful: it lets shoe soles grip the ground, lets us hold a pencil, and is how bicycle brakes stop the wheels. Letting the same car roll from the same place on different surfaces, as in the home experiment, is a fair test: only the surface changes, so any difference in distance comes from the surface.","play":"race a car down a ramp","steps":["Ask a grown-up to help you make a ramp: lean a big book against a cushion or a box.","Let a toy car roll down onto a smooth floor. Put a block where it stops.","Now spread a towel at the bottom of the ramp and roll the car again from the same place. Mark where it stops.","Where did it go further? Which floor rubbed more?"],"quiz":[["What slows a rolling car down?",["Sunshine","Rubbing","Singing"],1],["On which floor does the car roll the furthest?",["Grass","Carpet","Ice"],2],["Rubbing helps your shoes...",["Grip, so you don't slip","Change colour","Grow bigger"],0]],"color":"#E07A5F"},"ep17":{"idea":"Raindrops split the sunlight!","explain":"Sunlight looks white, but it is a mixture of all the colours. When light passes from air into water or glass it bends (refraction), and each colour bends by a slightly different amount: violet bends the most and red the least. That is why a glass prism spreads white light into a band of colours. A raindrop works in the same way: sunlight enters the drop and bends, reflects off the back of the drop, and bends again as it leaves, so the colours fan out. From each drop only one colour reaches your eye, so red reaches you from drops higher in the sky and violet from lower ones, which puts red on the outside of the bow and violet on the inside. You see a rainbow only with the sun behind you and rain in front of you, and it is easiest to see when the sun is low, in the morning or late afternoon. A rainbow is really a full circle around the point directly opposite the sun; the ground usually hides the lower part. Because it is made by whichever drops sit at the right angle from your own eyes, the rainbow moves when you move, you can never reach its end, and everyone sees their own rainbow. In the video the bending of the colours is drawn much bigger than it really is, so that it can be seen. More than 350 years ago, scientists used prisms to show that white sunlight is a mixture of colours. Sources: US National Weather Service, Flagstaff office (\"Rainbows\"); Australian Bureau of Meteorology (\"How do rainbows form?\").","play":"move the sun to make a rainbow appear, or split a beam of light with a prism and a raindrop","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a spray bottle of water on a sunny morning or late afternoon, when the sun is low.","Go outside and stand with the sun behind you, so your shadow points away from the sun. Never look straight at the sun.","Spray a fine mist in front of you and slowly move the spray up, down and around until the colours appear.","Can you make your own rainbow? Which colour is on the outside, and which is on the inside?"],"quiz":[["What colours are hiding in white sunlight?",["Only yellow","All the colours of the rainbow","Polka dots"],1],["Where must the sun be for you to see a rainbow?",["Right in front of you","In your pocket","Behind you, with rain in front"],2],["Which colour is on the outside (the top) of a rainbow?",["Red","Green","Violet"],0]],"color":"#9B6BFF"},"ep18":{"idea":"The Moon stays round, our view changes!","explain":"The Moon does not make its own light: moonlight is sunlight reflected off the Moon's surface. The Sun always lights up one half of the Moon (its day side), while the other half is dark (its night side). The Moon travels around the Earth in its orbit, and as it goes around we see different amounts of the sunlit half. When the dark side faces us we cannot see the Moon at all: a new Moon. Then the lit part we can see grows (waxing): a thin crescent, a half (first quarter), a gibbous Moon and the full Moon, when the whole sunlit side faces the Earth. Then it shrinks again (waning) through a half (third quarter) and a crescent back to a new Moon. From the northern half of the Earth, including India, the growing Moon is bright on the right and the shrinking Moon bright on the left. The Moon's shape never changes; only our view of its sunlit half does. The cycle from one new Moon to the next takes about 29.5 days (one trip around the Earth takes about 27.3 days). The Moon can often be seen in the daytime, too. Sources: NASA Space Place (\"What Are the Moon's Phases?\") and NASA Science (\"Moon Phases\").","play":"drag the Moon around the Earth and watch its phase, or step night by night through a whole month of Moons","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a lamp with its shade taken off, and a ball (an orange works too), and make the room dark.","The grown-up puts the lamp on a table at one side of the room. Don't touch the bulb (it can get warm), and don't look straight into the light.","Clear a space so you don't bump into things. Hold the ball up at arm's length, a little above your head, and turn slowly to your left in a full circle, watching the ball all the time.","When is the ball dark, half bright, or all bright? Can you make a new Moon, a crescent and a full Moon?"],"quiz":[["Where does moonlight come from?",["The Moon makes its own light","Sunlight bouncing off the Moon","Lots of tiny torches"],1],["In the model, what was the ball?",["The Moon","The sun","A snowball"],0],["Can you sometimes see the Moon in the daytime?",["Never","Only in winter","Yes, often"],2]],"color":"#5B6BD8"},"ep19":{"idea":"Ask what would happen without it!","explain":"To know whether something caused an outcome, we need to compare what actually happened with what would have happened if, contrary to fact, that thing had been different. This 'what would have happened' is called the counterfactual (or potential) outcome. The ideal comparison is a thought experiment: the same people, over the same time, both with and without the exposure, with nothing else different. Rothman calls this the counterfactual ideal. It can never be achieved, because each person lives through only one of the two conditions: we observe one outcome and the other stays unknown. So, in practice, scientists substitute the experience of a real comparison group without the exposure, and the key question becomes whether that group really shows what would have happened to the exposed group. When it does not, the comparison is confounded. Study designs try to make the groups comparable: random assignment (as in trials of new medicines) tends to balance other characteristics when there are enough people; crossover studies compare people with themselves at different times; observational studies choose comparison groups with similar risk factors. Even then, a causal conclusion stays tentative and open to new evidence. In the video, picking names from a hat stands for random assignment, and the socks stand for an exposure. Sources: K. J. Rothman, Epidemiology: An Introduction, 3rd ed., Chapter 4 ('The Counterfactual Ideal'); T. L. Lash, T. J. VanderWeele, S. Haneuse and K. J. Rothman, Modern Epidemiology, 4th ed., Chapters 3 and 5.","play":"race Curie against a what-if Curie with different socks or an uphill road, or make two teams fairly by picking from a hat","steps":["Ask a grown-up to help you fold a paper plane.","Stand behind a line and throw it five times, the same way, from the same spot. Mark where it lands each time. Throw only into an open space, never towards anyone.","Now put a sticker on the same plane and throw it five more times, the same way, from the same spot. Mark where it lands.","Did the plane fly further with the sticker, or about the same? Why is it fairer to test the same plane both ways?"],"quiz":[["To know if the socks helped Curie win, what do we need to know?",["What colour the socks are","What would have happened without the socks","How many socks she has"],1],["How did the scientists try to make the two groups alike?",["They picked names from a hat","They put all the fast runners together","They let the runners choose"],0],["Who uses this test to find out if a new medicine really helps?",["Pirates","Bakers","Doctors"],2]],"color":"#FF5C8A"},"ep20":{"idea":"Alike in every way, except one!","explain":"In 1854 cholera struck many people in London. Cholera causes severe diarrhoea, so the body loses water fast. At the time most experts believed diseases like cholera spread through 'miasma', bad air from rotting matter, and the germ theory of disease was not yet accepted. John Snow (1813-1858), a London doctor who was also a pioneer of anaesthesia, had argued for several years that cholera was spread by drinking water contaminated with sewage. To test the idea he used what he called an experiment 'on the grandest scale': in parts of south London two companies piped water down the same streets. The Southwark and Vauxhall Company took Thames water contaminated by London's sewage, while the Lambeth Company had in 1852 moved its intake upstream to Thames Ditton. Households differed in water supply but not in wealth or occupation, and people often did not know which company supplied them, so Snow had to find out which company supplied each home. In 1854 the risk of fatal cholera was about 1.6% among Southwark and Vauxhall customers and 0.27% among Lambeth customers, about six times higher (Rothman, Table 5.1); these figures cover all of the two companies' customers in London, not only the streets where their pipes were intermixed. The video says 'cholera struck' for these fatal attacks. Studies like this, where circumstances create the comparison an experimenter would have made, are called natural experiments: the two groups are alike in every way that matters except the one thing being studied (the video's phrase, 'alike in every way, except one', simplifies this). Snow's work did not convince most of the medical establishment at the time, but today he is considered a founder of epidemiology. Curie's Time Bubble and her puppy Biscuit are pretend; everything about John Snow and the water companies is real. (Muddy Drop and Splashy Drop, the racing water drops, are pretend too.) Episode 25 tells the other half of the story: the Broad Street pump in Soho and its handle. Sources: K. J. Rothman, Epidemiology: An Introduction, 3rd ed., Chapters 2 and 5; T. L. Lash, T. J. VanderWeele, S. Haneuse and K. J. Rothman, Modern Epidemiology, 4th ed., Chapters 1 and 6; London Museum; LSHTM podcast (March 2013).","play":"switch houses between two water pipes and compare, or pick the fair way to test the water","steps":["Ask a grown-up for two clean cups that look the same, filled with the same drinking water, a clean spoon and a little sugar.","While you close your eyes, the grown-up stirs a small pinch of sugar into one cup with the clean spoon and mixes the cups around.","Take a small sip from each cup (if a friend joins in, each of you uses your own cups). They look alike in every way. What is different?","Which cup has the sugar? How did comparing two cups that are alike in every way, except one, help you find it?"],"quiz":[["What did John Snow think spread cholera?",["Bad, smelly air","Dirty water","Cats"],1],["Why was comparing next-door houses a fair test?",["The houses were alike, except for their water","The houses were all big","Everyone drank the same water"],0],["Where did cholera strike more often?",["In homes with cleaner water","In homes with no cats","In homes with the dirty river water"],2]],"color":"#3F5A4E"},"ep21":{"idea":"Safe bags make safe flights!","explain":"At the airport every cabin bag goes through an X-ray scanner. X-rays pass through the bag and are caught by a detector on the other side; dense materials such as metal absorb more of them, so they show up dark, while soft things such as clothes, paper and food let more through. Modern scanners use two X-ray energies to estimate what each object is made of and colour the picture: organic materials appear orange, metal and glass blue or green, and denser things darker (BBC Science Focus). Sharp objects such as knives and pointed metal scissors are not allowed in the cabin, because they could injure people in a confined space; they travel in checked baggage instead. Liquids are harder: on an X-ray picture many different liquids look very similar, and a few liquids are hazardous or flammable. Since 2007, following ICAO guidelines that most countries adopted, liquids, aerosols and gels in cabin baggage must be in containers of 100 ml or less, all in one clear, re-sealable bag of about 1 litre (the limit is the size of the container, so a big bottle that is only partly full is still not allowed); medicines and baby food are exempt (Airports Authority of India security information; Air India). Limiting the amount keeps any risk small, and the clear bag lets officers check the containers quickly. Newer 3D (CT) scanners can analyse liquids better, and some airports that have them, for example several UK airports in 2026, allow containers of up to 2 litres; rules differ between airports and countries, so check before you fly. In India the 100 ml rule applies. The torch activity is only an analogy: visible light passes through fewer things than X-rays do. Sources: Airports Authority of India, airport security information; Air India cabin baggage guidelines; ICAO guidelines on liquids, aerosols and gels (UN News, December 2006); BBC Science Focus, 'How does an airport scanner work?'; Which?, 'The UK's airport liquid rules for 2026'.","play":"pack a cabin bag and scan it, or look at everyday things with X-ray eyes","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a torch, a sheet of paper, a clear plastic bottle of water and a metal spoon.","Make the room dark and shine the torch at a plain wall. Never shine it into anyone's eyes.","Hold each thing, one at a time, between the torch and the wall. Look at the wall: a bright patch, a dim glow, or a dark shadow?","Which things let the light through, and which block it? The X-ray scanner at the airport works a bit like this, but X-rays can pass through much more than light can."],"quiz":[["On the X-ray scanner picture, why do the scissor blades look dark?",["They are very cold","Metal blocks the X-rays","The scissors are dirty"],1],["Where do sharp things like scissors travel when you fly?",["In the big suitcase, under the plane","In your pocket","In your cabin bag"],0],["How big can bottles be in your cabin bag at most airports?",["As big as a bucket","One litre each","100 millilitres or less"],2]],"color":"#9B6BFF"},"ep22":{"idea":"Air goes in, burps come out!","explain":"A burp is just gas leaving the stomach. Whenever we eat or drink we swallow some air with the food, and eating or drinking too fast, or drinking through a straw, sends extra air down. Fizzy drinks add even more gas: their fizz is carbon dioxide. The gas collects at the top of the stomach, and the extra gas is pushed back up the esophagus (the food tube) and out of the mouth. The noise comes from a ring of muscle at the top of the esophagus: the gas pushes through it and makes the throat vibrate, a bit like air squeaking out of a stretched balloon neck. Grown-ups pat a baby's back to help the burp come out, so the extra gas does not make the baby uncomfortable. Burping is normal. Slow sips mean fewer burps, and the polite thing is to cover your mouth and say excuse me. Sources: KidsHealth (Nemours), \"Why Do I Burp?\"; The Conversation, Curious Kids, \"Why do burps make noise?\".","play":"feed a tummy until it burps, or swirl plain and fizzy water under a balloon","steps":["Ask a grown-up to blow up a balloon and hold its neck closed. Balloons only with a grown-up: if a balloon pops, give all the pieces to a grown-up. Never put balloon pieces in your mouth.","Stretch the neck sideways with your fingers, so it makes a thin slit.","Let the air squeak out slowly. Pull the neck tighter or looser: does the sound change?","Talk about it: how is the squeaky balloon neck like the top of your food tube when you burp?"],"quiz":[["What is a burp?",["A tiny frog in your tummy","Gas coming back up out of your stomach","Food that forgot to go down"],1],["A balloon goes on a bottle of plain water and a bottle of fizzy water. Which balloon puffs up a little?",["Neither one","The plain water one","The fizzy water one"],2],["What do you do when you burp?",["Cover your mouth and say excuse me","Burp even louder","Flap your arms like a chicken"],0]],"color":"#FFB020"},"ep23":{"idea":"Steam inside makes it pop!","explain":"A popcorn kernel is a seed with a hard, almost waterproof outer shell (the hull, or pericarp) around a store of starch, and about 14% of it is water. When the kernel is heated, that water turns into steam. Steam takes up far more room than liquid water, and because the hull traps it, the pressure inside rises. At about 180 °C (much hotter than boiling water) the hull can no longer hold: it cracks, the steam rushes out, and the hot, softened (gelatinised) starch puffs out and sets into the white, fluffy flake. High-speed films by Emmanuel Virot and Alexandre Ponomarenko (2015) showed that the pop sound comes from the steam escaping, not from the hull cracking, and that a bulge of starch pushes against the pan like a little leg and makes the popcorn jump. A kernel with a cracked hull lets the steam leak out, and a kernel that has dried out has too little water, so neither builds enough pressure to pop well. The oldest popcorn found so far, from Paredones and Huaca Prieta on the north coast of Peru, is about 6,700 years old. Popping needs a hot pan, so it is a grown-up's job, with a lid on the pan. The paper-bag activity shows a related idea without heat: clapping squeezes the trapped air, and the squeezed air pushes on the paper until it bursts. Sources: Science Buddies, 'Explore the POP in popcorn'; E. Virot and A. Ponomarenko, Journal of the Royal Society Interface (2015), reported in Science News Explores, 'How popcorn got its pop'; Guinness World Records, 'Earliest popcorn'.","play":"heat a kernel until it pops, or test which kernels pop","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a paper bag. Never use a plastic bag.","Blow into the bag with a few big breaths (rest if you feel dizzy), then twist the top shut so the air can't get out.","Hold the bag away from everyone's faces and clap your hands on it hard. Pop! It's loud, so warn everyone first.","What burst the bag? Your clap squeezed the air inside, and the squeezed air pushed on the paper until it burst. In a popcorn kernel, heat turns the water into steam, and the steam pushes on the shell until it bursts."],"quiz":[["What makes a popcorn kernel pop?",["Tiny jumping beans","Steam pushing on the shell","A loud song"],1],["Scientists heated a kernel with a crack in its shell. What happened?",["The steam leaked out, so it didn't pop","It popped twice","It turned into a flower"],0],["Who should pop popcorn in a hot pan?",["A teddy bear","Little scientists on their own","A grown-up"],2]],"color":"#F2A93B"},"ep24":{"idea":"Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly!","explain":"Butterflies change completely as they grow. A butterfly lays tiny eggs on the plant its caterpillars will eat. A caterpillar (the larva) hatches, and a monarch caterpillar's first meal is its own eggshell; then it eats leaves and grows fast: a monarch caterpillar gets about 2,000 times heavier in about two weeks. Its skin cannot grow with it, so it sheds its skin several times (most caterpillars shed five times in all). The last time it sheds, a chrysalis (the pupa) is underneath. Inside, the caterpillar's body breaks down and is rebuilt into a butterfly, with wings, legs and antennae. This complete change of body is called metamorphosis. The butterfly comes out with wet, crumpled wings, pumps them up, lets them dry and flies away, and later lays eggs of its own. Sources: American Museum of Natural History, A guide to the butterfly life cycle; Butterfly Conservation, Life cycle of butterflies and moths; Journey North (monarch caterpillar growth).","play":"feed a hungry caterpillar until it becomes a butterfly, or put the butterfly life cycle in order","steps":["Go on a leaf hunt with a grown-up, in a garden or a park.","Ask the grown-up to gently turn over some leaves, and look underneath for tiny round eggs. Look, but don't touch!","Look for leaves with nibbled holes or bitten edges: a caterpillar may be eating nearby. Wash your hands when you get home.","Talk about it: what will a caterpillar become? What comes before, and after?"],"quiz":[["What does a caterpillar turn into?",["A worm","A butterfly","A bird"],1],["Why does a caterpillar wriggle out of its old skin?",["Its skin gets too tight as it grows","It wants a new colour","It is too cold"],0],["What is the order?",["Butterfly, egg, chrysalis, caterpillar","Chrysalis, egg, butterfly, caterpillar","Egg, caterpillar, chrysalis, butterfly"],2]],"color":"#F28C28"},"ep25":{"idea":"Follow the clues to find the cause!","explain":"This is part 2 of John Snow's story (part 1, Episode 20, is his comparison of two water companies; both happened in 1854). In late August 1854 a severe outbreak of cholera began in Soho, London; the London Museum counts about 500 deaths in ten days. Cholera causes severe diarrhoea, so the body loses water fast, and at the time most experts believed it spread through 'miasma', bad air. John Snow (1813-1858), a London doctor, had argued that cholera was spread by drinking water contaminated with sewage. Many people then fetched their water from public street pumps. Snow found that the deaths clustered around the pump in Broad Street and that most of the people who died had drunk its water, and he persuaded the Board of Guardians of St James's parish to remove the pump handle (8 September 1854). As Rothman notes, the outbreak was already waning when the handle came off (Snow's own daily counts of fatal attacks are drawn in the video and in Try it 2), so it is not clear how many cases the removal prevented, though it may have prevented a second wave. Later investigation showed that the brickwork around the pump was defective and that water from a cesspool next to it could enter the pump's well. In the video the map with dots stands for the information Snow collected (his famous published map was drawn a little later), and the narration says 'cholera struck' or 'became very ill' rather than speaking of deaths. Curie's Time Bubble and her puppy Biscuit are pretend; everything about John Snow and the Broad Street pump is real. Today Snow is considered the founding father of epidemiology. Sources: K. J. Rothman, Epidemiology: An Introduction, 3rd ed., Chapters 2 and 5; T. L. Lash, T. J. VanderWeele, S. Haneuse and K. J. Rothman, Modern Epidemiology, 4th ed., Chapter 1; London Museum, John Snow, cholera and the Broad Street pump; LSHTM podcast (March 2013).","play":"mark the homes where cholera struck and find the pump they crowd around, or add John Snow's daily counts bar by bar and take the pump handle off","steps":["Ask a grown-up for a sheet of paper and a pencil.","Together, draw a simple map of your kitchen floor from above: the walls, the table and the chairs.","Have a crumbly snack at the table together (one with no nuts). Afterwards, look for crumbs on the floor, and for every crumb you find, draw a dot on your map in the same place. Don't eat crumbs from the floor! Then sweep them up together.","Where are the most dots? What is near them? What do the dots tell you about where the crumbs came from?"],"quiz":[["How did John Snow find the pump that was making people ill?",["He asked his puppy","He put a dot on a map for each home where cholera struck","He smelled the air"],1],["Where did most of the dots on John Snow's map crowd together?",["Around the river","Under the Time Bubble","Near one pump, on Broad Street"],2],["What did the people in charge do?",["Took the handle off the pump, so nobody could use it","Painted the pump pink","Built a bigger pump"],0]],"color":"#3F5A4E"}}};